ble.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  General \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\).  \nConsider the following set of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{L_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}}_{\\text{sunny}} \\;\n\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{x=j\\mid j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}} .\n\\]\n\n*The sunny part.*  \nThe lines \\(L_i\\) are exactly those used in the construction for \\(k=n\\); they are sunny and distinct.\n\n*The non‑sunny part.*  \nThe vertical lines \\(x=j\\) (\\(j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\)) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they are distinct from each other and from the \\(L_i\\) because none of the \\(L_i\\) is vertical.\n\nWe verify that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge k+1\\), then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to the second part of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\), so the point is covered.\n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\), then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  b-a\\in\\{1-k,2-k,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  The number \\(b-a\\) equals the intercept \\(1-i\\) of the line \\(L_i\\) with \\(i=b-a+1\\) (which lies between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\)). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(S_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. No other values of \\(k\\) are possible because the number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is