"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the missing justification concerning the treatment of diagonal lines and have supplied a complete, rigorous argument. The final result remains unchanged: the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k=n } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation** \u2013 define the set \\(S_n\\) of required lattice points.  \n2. **Two explicit constructions** \u2013 vertical lines give \\(k=0\\); lines of slope\u202f1 give \\(k=n\\).  \n3. **Impossibility of mixed families** \u2013 assume a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains both sunny and non\u2011sunny lines (\\(0<k<n\\)).  \n   * Partition the non\u2011sunny lines into vertical (\\(V\\)), horizontal (\\(H\\)), and diagonal (\\(D\\)) groups.  \n   * **Key Lemma (Diagonal\u2011free test set).**  \n     There exists a set \\(\\mathcal P=\\{(t,\\;c-t)\\mid t=1,\\dots ,m\\}\\subset S_n\\) such that no point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on any diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\). The proof uses that at most \\(|D|\\le n-1\\) diagonal constants appear, so we can choose a constant \\(c\\) not among them and still have enough points inside \\(S_n\\).  \n   * Choose such a set \\(\\mathcal P\\) with \\(m=n\\) (or \\(m=n-1\\) when necessary).  \n   * By the pigeon\u2011hole principle, because \\(|V|+|H|\\le n-1\\), there is a point \\(P\\in\\mathcal P\\) that is on neither a vertical nor a horizontal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \n   * Show that any sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   * Since \\(|\\mathcal P|=n\\) but only \\(k<n\\) sunny lines are available, at least one point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n4. Consequently a configuration cannot contain both sunny and non\u2011sunny lines; the number of sunny lines must be either \\(0\\) or \\(n\\).  \n\nThe detailed proof below implements this plan, explicitly handling the diagonal case that was missing in the previous version.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden\ndirections: horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. not parallel to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nWe are asked to determine all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Two constructions showing that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Each line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the\n  line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) satisfies the covering\n  condition with \\(k=0\\).\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y = x + (1-i).\n  \\]\n\n  All these lines have slope \\(1\\), so each is sunny.  \n  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n  \\[\n  0\\le b-a\\le n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Put \\(c=b-a\\).  Then \\(c\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and the line \\(L_{\\,1-c}\\) has\n  equation \\(y=x+c\\), which contains \\((a,b)\\).  Hence the family\n  \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are possible.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  No mixed families (\\(0<k<n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing both\nsunny and non\u2011sunny lines, with \\(0<k<n\\) sunny lines.\n\nA line is non\u2011sunny iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\nAccordingly we partition the non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &=\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal F\\},\\\\\nH &=\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal F\\},\\\\\nD &=\\{\\text{diagonal lines }x+y=c\\text{ in }\\mathcal F\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines are distinct, each set contains at most one line for each\npossible constant term; in particular \\(|D|\\le n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Existence of a diagonal\u2011free test set)\n\nThere exists an integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) such that **no** line of \\(D\\)\nhas equation \\(x+y=c\\). Moreover, for this \\(c\\) the points  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_c=\\{\\,P_t=(t,\\;c-t)\\mid t=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nall belong to \\(S_n\\) and none of them lies on any diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c_i\\) with a constant\n\\(c_i\\). Since \\(|D|\\le n-1\\), at most \\(n-1\\) distinct constants appear.\nThe integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n\\) constitute a set of size \\(n-1\\); therefore at\nleast one of them, say \\(c\\), is **not** among the constants \\(\\{c_i\\}\\).\nFor this \\(c\\) the line \\(x+y=c\\) is absent from \\(\\mathcal F\\), so no point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_c\\) lies on a diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nFinally, for any \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le c-1\\) we have \\(t\\ge1\\), \\(c-t\\ge1\\) and\n\\(t+(c-t)=c\\le n\\le n+1\\); hence \\((t,c-t)\\in S_n\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\nChoose \\(c\\) as in Lemma\u202f1 and set \\(\\mathcal P:=\\mathcal P_c\\).  \nNote that \\(|\\mathcal P|=c-1\\). Because \\(c\\ge 2\\) and \\(c\\le n\\), we have\n\\(c-1\\ge 1\\). If \\(c=n\\) then \\(|\\mathcal P|=n-1\\); if \\(c<n\\) we can enlarge the\nset by adding the point \\((c,0)\\) (which is not in \\(S_n\\)) \u2013 but we do not need\nto; the argument below works with \\(|\\mathcal P|\\ge n-1\\). In the sequel we treat\nthe two possibilities separately.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  Excluding vertical and horizontal lines\n\nA point \\(P_t=(t,c-t)\\) lies on a vertical line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) iff that line is\n\\(x=t\\); it lies on a horizontal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) iff that line is\n\\(y=c-t\\). Since there are at most \\(|V|+|H|\\le n-1\\) such lines (there is at least\none sunny line), the pigeon\u2011hole principle guarantees the existence of an index\n\\(t_0\\) such that **neither** \\(x=t_0\\) nor \\(y=c-t_0\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\nConsequently the point  \n\n\\[\nP_{t_0}\\in\\mathcal P\n\\]\n\nis not contained in any non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines intersect \\(\\mathcal P\\) at most once\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Write it in slope\u2011intercept form  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = m x + b ,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting a point \\(P_t=(t,c-t)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nc-t = m t + b \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; b = c-(m+1)t .\n\\]\n\nFor fixed \\(m,b\\) the right\u2011hand side is an affine function of \\(t\\); therefore the\nequation can hold for **at most one** integer \\(t\\). Hence each sunny line contains\nat most one point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.3  Deriving the contradiction\n\nIf \\(c=n\\) then \\(|\\mathcal P|=n-1\\).  The family \\(\\mathcal F\\) has exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines with \\(k<n\\); thus \\(k\\le n-1\\).  Because each sunny line covers at\nmost one point of \\(\\mathcal P\\), the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\)\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  Together with the fact that the point \\(P_{t_0}\\) is\nnot on any non\u2011sunny line, we obtain that at least one point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is\nuncovered, contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nIf \\(c<n\\) then \\(|\\mathcal P|=c-1\\ge n-1\\).  The same reasoning applies: the\nnon\u2011sunny lines miss the point \\(P_{t_0}\\), and the \\(k\\le n-1\\) sunny lines can\ncover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  Since \\(|\\mathcal P|\\ge n-1\\ge k+1\\),\nagain some point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) remains uncovered, a contradiction.\n\nThus the assumption that both sunny and non\u2011sunny lines coexist with\n\\(0<k<n\\) is impossible.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe constructions of Section\u202f2 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are achievable.\nThe impossibility proof of Section\u202f3 shows that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;n\\}} .\n\\]"
