. Consequently a configuration cannot contain both sunny and non\u2011sunny lines; the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\nThe detailed proof below implements this plan, explicitly handling the diagonal case and providing the missing verification of coverage for the two constructions.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of required points  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points that must lie on at least one of the\n\\(n\\) lines.\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three\nforbidden directions: horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. not parallel\nto the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Two constructions showing that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,\\ell_i : x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Each \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n  the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers all points of\n  \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\; y = x + (1-i).\n  \\]\n\n  All these lines have slope \\(1\\), so each is sunny.  \n  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n  \\[\n  0\\le b-a\\le n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Put \\(c=b-a\\).  Then \\(c\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and the line \\(\\ell_{\\,1-c}\\) has\n  equation \\(y=x+c\\), which contains \\((a,b)\\).  Hence the family\n  \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny\n  lines.\n\nThus the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are indeed possible.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  No mixed families (\\(0<k<n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing both\nsunny and non\u2011sunny lines, with \\(0<k<n\\) sunny lines.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so we\npartition the non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &=\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal F\\},\\\\\nH &=\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal F\\},\\\\\nD &=\\{\\text{diagonal lines }x+y=c\\text{ in }\\mathcal F\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines are distinct, each set contains at most one line for each\npossible constant term; in particular \\(|D|\\le n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Diagonal\u2011free set of \\(n\\) points)\n\nThere exists a set \\(\\mathcal Q\\subset S_n\\) with \\(|\\mathcal Q|=n\\) such that\nno point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) lies on any diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) has equation \\(x+y=c_i\\) for some integer\n\\(c_i\\).  The constants \\(c_i\\) belong to the interval \\([2,n]\\) because any\npoint of \\(S_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\).  Since \\(|D|\\le n-1\\), at most\n\\(n-1\\) distinct constants appear among the diagonal lines.\n\nChoose an integer  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c_i\\mid x+y=c_i\\in D\\}.\n\\]\n\nSuch a \\(c\\) exists because we are removing at most \\(n-1\\) numbers from a set\nof size \\(n-1\\).  For this \\(c\\) the line \\(x+y=c\\) is **absent** from \\(\\mathcal\nF\\).\n\nConsider the points  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_c=\\{\\,P_t=(t,\\;c-t)\\mid t=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(P_t\\) satisfies \\(t\\ge1\\), \\(c-t\\ge1\\) and \\(t+(c-t)=c\\le n\\); hence\n\\(P_t\\in S_n\\).  Moreover, because the line \\(x+y=c\\) does not belong to\n\\(\\mathcal F\\), none of the points of \\(\\mathcal P_c\\) lies on a diagonal line\nof \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nIf \\(c=n\\) then \\(|\\mathcal P_c|=n-1\\).  In this case add the point\n\\((n,1)\\) (which also belongs to \\(S_n\\) and is not on any diagonal line,\nbecause its sum is \\(n+1\\neq c\\)).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q:=\\mathcal P_c\\cup\\{(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow \\(|\\mathcal Q|=n\\) and still no point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) lies on a diagonal\nline of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  If \\(c<n\\) then \\(|\\mathcal P_c|=c-1\\ge n-1\\); we can\nsimilarly enlarge \\(\\mathcal P_c\\) by adding any extra point of \\(S_n\\) whose\ncoordinate sum is different from \\(c\\) (for instance \\((n,1)\\)).  In all cases we\nobtain a set \\(\\mathcal Q\\subset S_n\\) with \\(|\\mathcal Q|=n\\) and free of\ndiagonal lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Vertical / horizontal lines cannot cover all of \\(\\mathcal Q\\))\n\nBecause the family contains at most \\(|V|+|H|\\le n-1\\) vertical or horizontal\nlines, the pigeon\u2011hole principle guarantees the existence of a point  \n\n\\[\nQ\\in\\mathcal Q\n\\]\n\nthat is **not** on any vertical line of \\(V\\) and not on any horizontal line of\n\\(H\\).  Indeed, each vertical line can contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal\nQ\\) (all points of a vertical line have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate), and similarly\neach horizontal line can contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\).  With at\nmost \\(n-1\\) such lines and \\(n\\) points, at least one point remains uncovered.\n\nThus \\(Q\\) is not on any non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (A sunny line meets \\(\\mathcal Q\\) at most once)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Write it in slope\u2011intercept form  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = m x + b ,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\in\\mathcal Q\\) satisfies the equation, then  \n\n\\[\ny = m x + b \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; b = y - m x .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq -1\\), the expression \\(y-mx\\) is an affine function of \\(x\\) with\nnon\u2011zero coefficient \\((-m-1)\\).  Consequently the equation can