al L_0\\) covers all points of\n  \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\; y = x + (1-i).\n  \\]\n\n  All these lines have slope \\(1\\), so each is sunny.  \n  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n  \\[\n  0\\le b-a\\le n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Put \\(c=b-a\\).  Then \\(c\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and the line \\(\\ell_{\\,1-c}\\)\n  has equation \\(y=x+c\\), which contains \\((a,b)\\).  Hence the family\n  \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny\n  lines.\n\nThus the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are indeed possible.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  No mixed families (\\(0<k<n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing both\nsunny and non‑sunny lines, with \\(0<k<n\\) sunny lines.\n\nA non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so we\npartition the non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &=\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal F\\},\\\\\nH &=\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal F\\},\\\\\nD &=\\{\\text{diagonal lines }x+y=c\\text{ in }\\mathcal F\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines are distinct, each set contains at most one line for each\npossible constant term; in particular \\(|D|\\le n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Existence of a diagonal‑free test line)\n\nThere exists an integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) such that the diagonal line\n\\(x+y=c\\) does **not** belong to \\(D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEvery diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) has the form \\(x+y=c_i\\) with an integer\n\\(c_i\\).  Any point of \\(S_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently each\n\\(c_i\\) lies in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\).  Since \\(|D|\\le n-1\\), at most\n\\(n-1\\) distinct integers are used among the \\(c_i\\).  The set\n\\(\\{2,3,