            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of required points  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points that must lie on at least one of the\n\\(n\\) lines.\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three\nforbidden directions: horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. not parallel\nto the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Two constructions showing that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,\\ell_i : x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Each \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \n  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n  the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers all points of\n  \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\; y = x + (1-i).\n  \\]\n\n  All these lines have slope \\(1\\), so each is sunny.  \n  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n  \\[\n  0\\le b-a\\le n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Put \\(c=b-a\\).  Then \\(c\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and the line \\(\\ell_{\\,1-c}\\)\n  has equation \\(y=x+c\\), which contains \\((a,b)\\).  Hence the family\n  \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny\n  lines.\n\nThus the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are indeed possible.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  No mixed families (\\(0<k<n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing both\nsunny and non‑sunny lines, with \\(0<k<n\\) sunny lines.\n\nA non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so we\npartition the non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &=\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal F\\},\\\\\nH &=\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal F\\},\\\\\nD &=\\{\\text{diagonal lines }x+y=c\\text{ in }\\mathcal F\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines are distinct, each set contains at most one line for each\npossible constant term; in particular \\(|D|\\le n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Existence of a diagonal‑free test line)\n\nThere exists an integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) such that the diagonal line\n\\(x+y=c\\) does **not** belong to \\(D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEvery diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) has the form \\(x+y=c_i\\) with an integer\n\\(c_i\\).  Any point of \\(S_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently each\n\\(c_i\\) lies in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\).  Since \\(|D|\\le n-1\\), at most\n\\(n-1\\) distinct integers are used among the \\(c_i\\).  The set\n\\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) integers, so at least one of them\nis absent from \\(\\{c_i\\}\\).  Choose such a missing integer and call it \\(c\\).\nThen the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) is not present in \\(D\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Construction of the test set \\(\\mathcal Q\\)\n\nLet \\(c\\) be given by Lemma 1.  Consider the lattice points of \\(S_n\\) that lie\non this diagonal:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_c=\\{\\, (t,\\,c-t)\\mid t=1,2,\\dots ,c-1 \\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach point satisfies \\(t\\ge1\\), \\(c-t\\ge1\\) and \\(t+(c-t)=c\\le n\\); hence\n\\(\\mathcal P_c\\subset S_n\\).  Moreover, by construction none of these points\nlies on a diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal P_c|=c-1=n\\) we set \\(\\mathcal Q:=\\mathcal P_c\\).  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal P_c|<n\\) (which can happen only when \\(c<n\\)), we enlarge the\nset by adding arbitrary points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinate sum is different\nfrom \\(c\\); for definiteness we add the point \\((n,1)\\) (its sum is \\(n+1\\neq\nc\\)).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q:=\\mathcal P_c\\cup\\{(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow \\(\\mathcal Q\\subset S_n\\), \\(|\\mathcal Q|=n\\), and **no point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\)**\nlies on any diagonal line of the family \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Vertical / horizontal lines cannot cover all of \\(\\mathcal Q\\))\n\nEach vertical line of \\(V\\) contains points with a single \\(x\\)-coordinate,\nhence can contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\); similarly each horizontal\nline of \\(H\\) can contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\).  Because  \n\n\\[\n|V|+|H|\\le n-1,\n\\]\n\nthe pigeon‑hole principle guarantees the existence of a point  \n\n\\[\nQ\\in\\mathcal Q\n\\]\n\nthat is **not** on any line of \\(V\\cup H\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 3 (A sunny line meets the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) at most once)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Its slope is different from \\(-1\\); therefore it\nis not parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).  Two distinct points of the diagonal\ndetermine a line of slope \\(-1\\), so a line with a different slope can intersect\nthe diagonal in at most one point.  Consequently \\(\\ell\\) contains at most one\npoint of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) (recall that all points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) that lie on\nthe diagonal have that diagonal as their unique line of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### Deriving the contradiction\n\nThe point \\(Q\\) supplied by Lemma 2 is not on any vertical or horizontal line\nof \\(\\mathcal F\\), and by construction it is not on any diagonal line of\n\\(\\mathcal F\\).  Hence the only lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) that could cover \\(Q\\)\nare the sunny lines.  There are exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, with \\(k<n\\) by\nassumption.  Lemma 3 shows that each sunny line can cover **at most one**\npoint of \\(\\mathcal Q\\); therefore the sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points\nof \\(\\mathcal Q\\).  Since \\(|\\mathcal Q|=n>k\\), at least one point of\n\\(\\mathcal Q\\) (in particular the point \\(Q\\)) remains uncovered, contradicting\nthe requirement that every point of \\(S_n\\) be covered.\n\nThus a family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the covering condition cannot contain\nboth sunny and non‑sunny lines.  The number of sunny lines must be either\n\\(0\\) or \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Final answer  \n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k=n } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
