\) is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\), then the admissible points on \\(L\\) are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); the largest possible value of \\(n+1-c\\) is \\(n\\) (attained for \\(c=1\\)).  \nThe vertical case is symmetric.  \nIf \\(L\\) is diagonal, \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), then the admissible points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); the largest possible value of \\(c-1\\) is \\(n\\) (attained for \\(c=n+1\\)). \u220e\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2 (Upper bound for many sunny lines).**  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition of the problem and contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Then the total number of incidences between the lines of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) and the set\n\\[\n   S=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb{Z}^2\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\; a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\nis at most \\(2n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \nEach sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, hence it can intersect any non\u2011sunny line in at most one point, and it cannot contain two distinct points of \\(S\\) because any two points of \\(S\\) have either equal \\(x\\)-coordinates, equal \\(y\\)-coordinates, or sum \\(x+y\\) equal to a constant, which would make the line parallel to a forbidden direction. Consequently each sunny line contributes at most one incidence with \\(S\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences.  \nHence the total number of incidences is at most\n\\[\n   s\\cdot 1 + t\\cdot n = s + (n-s)n = n + s(1-n) \\le n + (n-1)(1-n)=2n-1,\n\\]\nwhere the last inequality uses \\(s\\ge n-1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**Corollary 3 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)).**  \nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) there is no configuration of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the covering condition with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(S\\) contains \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \nIf a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) existed, Lemma\u202f2 would give at most \\(2n-1\\) incidences, but\n\\[\n   2n-1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\nso the points of \\(S\\) could not all be covered \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y=i \\quad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three families are non\u2011sunny (horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\), respectively).  \nWe verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), i.e. on \\(L_b\\).\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a=1\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so \\((1,n-1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (= \\(L_{n-1}\\)).\n* If \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) again, and the point \\((1,n)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\), hence lies on \\(L_n\\).\n\nThus the covering condition holds and the configuration contains **no** sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n**Adding sunny lines.**  \nStarting from the above configuration, replace any horizontal line \\(y=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\)) by a sunny line that passes through the same set of points \\(\\{(a,i)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-i\\}\\).  \nFor instance, the line through \\((1,i)\\) and \\((n+1-i,i)\\) has slope \\(0\\), so we must tilt it slightly: choose a line with equation\n\\[\n   y = i + \\varepsilon (x-1)\n\\]\nwith a small irrational \\(\\varepsilon\\neq 0,-1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\varepsilon\\) is irrational, the line contains no other lattice point of \\(S\\) besides \\((1,i)\\) and \\((n+1-i,i)\\); nevertheless those two points are already covered by the original horizontal line, so the covering property is preserved.  \nRepeating this replacement for any subset of the \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines yields configurations with any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfying\n\\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be achieved, while \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Conclusion (partial).**  \nWe have proved that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers\n\\[\n   0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-2 .\n\\]\nThe cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) cannot occur. A full solution would require showing that no other hidden restrictions eliminate any of the values \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\); the construction above demonstrates that all these values are indeed attainable."
