oint is on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (\\(L_{n-1}\\)).  \nIf \\(a=2\\) then \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and lies on \\(L_n\\).\n\n*Case 3: \\(b=n\\).*  \nThen necessarily \\(a=1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \nThus \\((1,n)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and lies on \\(L_n\\).\n\nAll possibilities are covered, so the covering condition holds.  \nSince none of the lines is sunny, this configuration realises \\(k=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### Remarks on the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe arguments above establish that\n\n\\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-2 \\quad\\text{is not ruled out,}\n\\]\nwhile \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.  \nA construction that yields configurations with a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines for each \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) has not been found, nor has an impossibility proof for any of those intermediate values. Consequently the complete description of the admissible \\(k\\) remains open. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
