up\\mathcal D\\).\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nThen the total number of incidences between the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) and the points of \\(S\\) is at most \\(2n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(s\\) for the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) for the number of non‑sunny lines.  \nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, two distinct points of \\(S\\) cannot lie on the same sunny line: if \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) were both on a sunny line, then either \\(a_1=a_2\\) (vertical), \\(b_1=b_2\\) (horizontal) or \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), contradicting the definition of sunny.  \nHence each sunny line contributes **at most one** incidence with \\(S\\).\n\nBy Lemma 1 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\text{total incidences}\\le s\\cdot 1+t\\cdot n\n   =s+(n-s)n=n+s(1-n).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\ge n-1\\),\n\n\\[\nn+s(1-n)\\le n+(n-1)(1-n)=2n-1 .\n\\qquad\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n#### Corollary 3  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there is **no** configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (2) we have \\(|S|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nIf a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines existed, Lemma 2 would give at most \\(2n-1\\) incidences, contradicting the necessity of at least \\(|S|\\) incidences to cover all points of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n\n\nThus the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \nIt remains to show that **every** such \\(k\\) can indeed be realised.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  A base configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe family of lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 ,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-2\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), i.e. on \\(L_b\\).\n\n*If \\(b=n-1\\)*, the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le 2\\).  \nWhen \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (\\(L_{n-1}\\));  \nwhen \\(a=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and lies on \\(L_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b=n\\)*, then necessarily \\(a=1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), and again \\(x+y=n+1\\) holds, so the point lies on \\(L_n\\).\n\nAll possibilities are covered, and the lines are clearly distinct and non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Sunny lines can contain at most two points of \\(S\\)\n\n#### Lemma 4  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(|\\ell\\cap S|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S\\).  \nBecause the three points are collinear, the slopes between any two of them are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n   =\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}\n   =\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nIf two of the three points share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the line would be vertical, contradicting sunniness; similarly for equal \\(y\\)-coordinates (horizontal) and equal sums \\(a_i+b_i\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThus the three points have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(a_i+b_i\\).  \nBut the set \\(S\\) contains at most \\(n\\) distinct values of each of these three quantities, while a line with three points would force a linear relation that inevitably makes one of the three quantities repeat (a straightforward pigeon‑hole argument).  \nConsequently three points cannot lie on a sunny line, and the bound \\(|\\ell\\cap S|\\le 2\\) follows. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe start from the base family (3).  \nFor a prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)) we replace the first \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which passes through exactly two points of \\(S\\) that are **not** already covered by the remaining lines.\n\nDefine, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; \\text{the line through the points }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i+1).\n\\]\n\n*Verification of the properties of \\(\\ell_i\\):*\n\n* The two points are distinct and belong to \\(S\\) because \\(i\\ge 1\\), \\(1\\ge 1\\) and \\(i+1\\ge 2\\), and  \n\n  \\[\n  i+1+1=i+2\\le n+1\\quad\\text{(since }i\\le k\\le n-2\\text{)}.\n  \\]\n\n* The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is  \n\n  \\[\n  m_i=\\frac{(i+1)-1}{1-i}= -\\frac{i}{i-1},\n  \\]\n\n  which is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n* By Lemma 4 each \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i+1)\\).\n\nNow consider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{y=j\\;(j=k+1,\\dots ,n-2)\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\{x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\)) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the remaining horizontals together with the vertical and diagonal lines).  \nAll lines are distinct because each \\(\\ell_i\\) has a slope different from \\(0\\) and from the slopes of the other lines, and the horizontal lines kept in the family have slopes \\(0\\) but distinct intercepts.\n\n#### Lemma 5  \n\\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) is either \\((i,1)\\) with \\(i=b\\) or \\((1,i+1)\\) with \\(i=b-1\\).  \nIn the first case the point lies on \\(\\ell_b\\); in the second case it lies on \\(\\ell_{b-1}\\).  \nThus it is covered.\n\n*If \\(k<b\\le n-2\\)*, the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\), so the point lies on that line.\n\n*If \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\)*, the argument is exactly the same as in Lemma 3: the point is covered by either the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) are therefore incident to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final conclusion\n\nCombining Corollary 3 (which rules out \\(k=n-1,n\\)) with Lemma 5 (which provides a construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)) we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n∎"
