  \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, contains exactly the two points shown in (4), and\n   those two points are not covered by any other line of the family.\n   The resulting family\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\n   \\bigl\\{\\ell_i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\bigl\\{y=j\\;(j=k+1,\\dots ,n-2)\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\{x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all distinct,\n   and Lemma 4 proves that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) still covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Corollary 2 excludes \\(k\\ge n-1\\); Lemma 3 and Lemma 4 provide explicit\n   configurations for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).  Hence the set of admissible\n   values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The required lattice points\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the line \\(a+b=s\\) contains\nexactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(S\\).  Summing over \\(s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  How many points can a sunny line contain?\n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or has\nslope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  All other lines are *sunny*.\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(\\ell\\) in the form \\(y=mx+b\\) with\n\\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) rational (otherwise \\(\\ell\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2}\\) is empty).\nLet \\(m=\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) be reduced with \\(q>0\\).  \nIf \\((x_0,y_0)\\in\\ell\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2}\\) then all integer points of \\(\\ell\\) are\n\\[\n(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(S\\) satisfies \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le y\\le n\\),\nfrom (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n1\\le x_0+qt\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le y_0+pt\\le n .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{x_0-1}{q}\\le t\\le\\frac{n-x_0}{q},\\qquad\n-\\frac{y_0-1}{p}\\le t\\le\\frac{n-y_0}{p}\n\\]\n\n(the second pair is interpreted in the obvious way when \\(p<0\\)).\nBoth intervals have length at most \\(\\dfrac{n-1}{|q|}\\) and\n\\(\\dfrac{n-1}{|p|}\\) respectively; consequently the set of admissible\nintegers \\(t\\) has cardinality at most  \n\n\\[\n\\min\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n          \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}+1\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n(The last inequality follows because \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\) for any reduced\nfraction with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).)  Thus a sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most\n\\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\nFor completeness we note the trivial bound for a non‑sunny line.\n\n#### Lemma 1′  \nIf \\(L\\) is horizontal, vertical or of the form \\(x+y=c\\), then\n\\(|L\\cap S|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Direct inspection of the three families (the longest possible\nintersection occurs for \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\), each containing exactly\n\\(n\\) points of \\(S\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Impossibility of many sunny lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers\n\\(S\\) and contains \\(s\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le s\\le n\\)).  Let \\(t=n-s\\) be the\nnumber of non‑sunny lines.\n\nFrom Lemma 1 and Lemma 1′ we obtain an upper bound for the total number of\nincidences between the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) and the points of \\(S\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{incidences}\n   &\\le s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n      +t\\cdot n\n   = s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n      +(n-s)n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(S\\) must be incident to at least one line, the number\nof incidences is at least \\(|S|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) (see (1)).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n                +(n-s)n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSolving (4) for \\(s\\) gives\n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for \\(s=n-1\\) the right‑hand side of (4) equals\n\\((n-1)\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+n\\), which is strictly smaller than\n\\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\); the same holds for \\(s=n\\).\n\nThus we have proved:\n\n#### Corollary 2  \nFor any \\(n\\ge3\\) a configuration satisfying the statement of the problem\ncannot contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  A covering with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll of them belong to the non‑sunny families, hence are distinct.\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which is \\(L_b\\)) contains the\npoint.\n\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le 2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n  – When \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (\\(L_{n-1}\\)).  \n  – When \\(a=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line\n    \\(x+y=n+1\\) (\\(L_n\\)).\n\n* If \\(b=n\\) then necessarily \\(a=1\\); again the point satisfies\n  \\(x+y=n+1\\) and lies on \\(L_n\\).\n\nAll possibilities are covered, proving the lemma. ∎\n\n\n\nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Adding sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;\\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i+1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe two points are distinct elements of \\(S\\) because\n\\(i\\ge1\\), \\(i+1\\ge2\\) and \\(i+1+1=i+2\\le n+1\\) (the latter uses \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\)).\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i-1}\\), which is different from\n\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we know that a sunny line can contain at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\); the line \\(\\ell_i\\) actually\ncontains exactly the two points displayed in (6).\n\nNow replace in (5) the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\) by the sunny lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  The resulting family is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\bigl\\{y=j\\;(j=k+1,\\dots ,n-2)\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\nall distinct (the slopes of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise different and none of\nthem equals \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\)).\n\n#### Lemma 4  \n\\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le k\\).*  \n  – When \\(b=i\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) the point is \\((a,i)\\).  \n    If \\(a=i\\) then \\((a,b)=(i,i)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\) because \\(\\ell_i\\) passes\n    through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i+1)\\) and therefore also through the midpoint\n    \\((i,i)\\).  \n    If \\(a\\neq i\\) then \\(a=1\\) (otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) would give\n    \\(a+b\\ge i+2\\le k+2\\le n+1\\) and the point would already be on the\n    horizontal line \\(y=i\\), which has been removed; but in that case\n    \\((1,i)\\) is precisely one of the two defining points of \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n    Hence the point belongs to \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n*If \\(k<b\\le n-2\\).*  \n  The horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\); thus the\n  point lies on that line.\n\n*If \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\).*  \n  The same argument as in Lemma 3 applies: the point is covered by the\n  vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) are therefore incident to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal F_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Final conclusion\n\nCorollary 2 shows that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible, while Lemma 3 and Lemma 4\nprovide explicit constructions for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).  Hence the set of all\nnon‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n∎"
