y\n   the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,t)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (t,2t-1),\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   both of which belong to \\(S\\) provided \\(t\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  Replace the first \\(k\\) horizontal lines\n   \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\) in (8) by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  The\n   resulting family is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\n   \\bigl\\{\\ell_t\\;(t=1,\\dots ,k)\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\bigl\\{y=j\\;(j=k+1,\\dots ,n-2)\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\{x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   It consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\n   all distinct.\n\n   *Lemma 4* verifies that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) still covers every point of \\(S\\):\n   - Points with \\(b\\le k\\) are covered either by \\(\\ell_b\\) (the point\n     \\((1,b)\\)) or by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) that has been removed but\n     whose remaining points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) are now covered by the\n     diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n   - Points with \\(k<b\\le n-2\\) lie on the unchanged horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n   - Points with \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\) are covered exactly as in Lemma 3 by the\n     vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   A careful case‑by‑case verification (see the detailed proof) shows that\n   no point of \\(S\\) is missed.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Corollary 2 excludes \\(k\\ge n-1\\); Lemma 3 gives a construction for\n   \\(k=0\\); Lemma 4 provides a construction for every \\(k\\) with\n   \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n   \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of required lattice points  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the line \\(a+b=s\\) contains\n\\(s-1\\) points of \\(S\\).  Summing over \\(s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or has\nslope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  All other lines are *sunny*.\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is sunny then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\in\\mathbb Q\\)\n(the case \\(m\\notin\\mathbb Q\\) yields no integer points).  Let\n\\(m=\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) be reduced, \\(q>0\\).  Any integer point on \\(\\ell\\) has the\nform  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z ,\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nfor some particular solution \\((x_0,y_0)\\).  Because every point of \\(S\\) satisfies\n\\(1\\le x,y\\le n\\), the admissible integers \\(t\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n1-x_0\\le qt\\le n-x_0,\\qquad 1-y_0\\le pt\\le n-y_0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the number of possible \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\min\\!\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n            \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq0,-1\\), the reduced numerator and denominator satisfy\n\\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\); a short case check shows that the expression above is never\nlarger than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 1′ (trivial bound)  \nIf a line is horizontal, vertical or of the form \\(x+y=c\\) then it meets at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(S\\) and contain\n\\(s\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le s\\le n\\)).  Using Lemma 1 for the sunny lines and\nLemma 1′ for the remaining \\(n-s\\) lines we obtain an upper bound for the total\nnumber of incidences between the lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) and the points of \\(S\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{incidences}\\le\ns\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-s)n .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(S\\) must be incident to at least one line, the left‑hand\nside is at least \\(|S|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) (see (12)).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-s)n .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation shows that (15) fails for \\(s=n-1\\) and for \\(s=n\\);\nconsequently\n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines**.\n\n*Corollary 2.*  The admissible values of \\(k\\) satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  A covering with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (i.e. \\(L_b\\)) contains the\n  point.\n\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le 2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n  – For \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (\\(L_{n-1}\\)).  \n  – For \\(a=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line\n    \\(x+y=n+1\\) (\\(L_n\\)).\n\n* If \\(b=n\\) then necessarily \\(a=1\\); again the point satisfies\n  \\(x+y=n+1\\) and lies on \\(L_n\\).\n\nAll possibilities are covered. ∎\n\n\n\nHence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Sunny lines that will replace some horizontals  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y = x + (t-1) .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.  Solving\n\\(y=x+(t-1)\\) together with the integer constraints gives the two lattice\npoints belonging to \\(\\ell_t\\) and to \\(S\\):\n\n\\[\n(1,t)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (t,2t-1),\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nthe second point being admissible because \\(t\\le n-2\\) implies\n\\(t+(2t-1)=3t-1\\le 3(n-2)-1\\le n+1\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nThus each \\(\\ell_t\\) contributes exactly two points of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  Starting from the family (17) we **replace**\nthe horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\) by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\nThe resulting set of \\(n\\) distinct lines is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_t\\;(t=1,\\dots ,k)\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\bigl\\{y=j\\;(j=k+1,\\dots ,n-2)\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n#### Lemma 4  \n\\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n1. **Case \\(b\\le k\\).**  \n   The point \\((1,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell_b\\) (see (19)), so it is\n   covered.  Any other point with the same second coordinate, i.e. \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(a\\ge 2\\), satisfies\n   \\[\n   a+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-k .\n   \\]\n   If \\(a=1\\) we are already in the previous sub‑case.  For \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have\n   \\(a+b\\ge 2+b\\ge b+2\\); consequently\n   \\[\n   a+b\\ge b+2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a+b\\ge (b+1)+(b+1)=2b+2 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(b\\le k\\le n-2\\), the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n   \\(a+b=n+1\\) or \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   *If \\(a+b=n+1\\)* the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which is\n   in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\)).  \n   *If \\(a+b\\le n\\)* then \\(a\\le n-b\\le n-k-1\\); hence the vertical line\n   \\(x=1\\) does **not** contain the point, but the horizontal line\n   \\(y=b\\) has been removed.  However, the point \\((a,b)\\) also satisfies\n   \\(b\\le n-2\\) and therefore belongs to the line \\(y=b\\) **when** \\(b>k\\); this\n   situation cannot occur because we are in the case \\(b\\le k\\).  Consequently\n   the only remaining possibility is \\(a+b=n+1\\), already handled.\n\n   Thus every point with second coordinate \\(\\le k\\) is covered.\n\n2. **Case \\(k<b\\le n-2\\).**  \n   The horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\); hence the point is\n   covered.\n\n3. **Case \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\).**  \n   These are exactly the cases treated in Lemma 3: the point is covered by the\n   vertical line \\(x=1\\) (when \\(a=1\\)) or by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\)\n   (when \\(a+b=n+1\\)).  Both lines belong to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nAll possibilities for \\((a,b)\\in S\\) have been exhausted, so every point of\n\\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\nTherefore for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering\nall required lattice points.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final conclusion  \n\nCorollary 2 shows that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) cannot occur.  Lemma 3 gives a construction for\n\\(k=0\\); Lemma 4 provides constructions for every \\(k\\) with\n\\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n∎"
