ngs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion** \u2013 Corollary\u202f2 excludes \\(k\\ge n-1\\); Lemma\u202f3 gives a construction\n   for \\(k=0\\); Lemma\u202f4 provides constructions for all \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of required lattice points  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the line \\(a+b=s\\) contains\n\\(s-1\\) points of \\(S\\).  Summing gives  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or has\nslope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  All other lines are *sunny*.\n\n#### Lemma\u202f1  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is sunny then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with rational slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) (if \\(m\\) is\nirrational the line contains no lattice point).  Let \\(m=\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) be in\nlowest terms, \\(q>0\\).  Any integer point on \\(\\ell\\) has the form  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nfor a particular solution \\((x_0,y_0)\\).  The conditions \\(1\\le x,y\\le n\\) give  \n\n\\[\n1-x_0\\le qt\\le n-x_0,\\qquad 1-y_0\\le pt\\le n-y_0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the admissible integers \\(t\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nt\\in I:=\\Bigl[\\max\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{1-x_0}{q},\\frac{1-y_0}{p}\\Bigr),\n            \\min\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{n-x_0}{q},\\frac{n-y_0}{p}\\Bigr)\\Bigr]\\cap\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nThe length of \\(I\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\min\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{n-1}{|q|},\\frac{n-1}{|p|}\\Bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq0,-1\\), the reduced numerator and denominator satisfy\n\\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\).  A short case analysis (four possibilities for the signs of\n\\(p,q\\)) shows that the right\u2011hand side of (14) never exceeds\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Therefore\n\\(|\\ell\\cap S|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma\u202f1\u2032 (trivial bound)  \nIf a line is horizontal, vertical or of the form \\(x+y=c\\) then it meets at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(S\\) and contain\n\\(s\\) sunny lines.  Using Lemma\u202f1 for the sunny lines and Lemma\u202f1\u2032 for the\nremaining \\(n-s\\) lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S|\n   \\le s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-s)n .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSolving (15) for \\(s\\) yields \\(s\\le n-2\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\le n-2}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n*Corollary\u202f2.*  No configuration can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  A covering with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nAll are non\u2011sunny.\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3  \nThe family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (i.e. \\(L_b\\)) contains the\n  point.\n\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n  \u2013 For \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(x=1\\) (\\(L_{n-1}\\)).  \n  \u2013 For \\(a=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line\n    \\(x+y=n+1\\) (\\(L_n\\)).\n\n* If \\(b=n\\) then necessarily \\(a=1\\); the point satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and lies\n  on \\(L_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n\n\nHence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Sunny lines that will replace some horizontals  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\ge1\\) we construct a sunny line \\(\\sigma_t\\) that passes\nthrough \\((1,t)\\) and another point of \\(S\\).\n\n*If \\(t\\neq\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) (this never happens when \\(n\\) is even), define*  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_t:\\;\\text{the line through }(1,t)\\text{ and }(t,n+1-t).\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThe second point belongs to \\(S\\) because its coordinates are positive and sum to\n\\(n+1\\).  The slope of \\(\\sigma_t\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_t=\\frac{n+1-2t}{t-1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\ge2\\) in this case, \\(m_t\\) is finite.  The equality \\(m_t=0\\) would give\n\\(n+1=2t\\), i.e. \\(t=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), which we have excluded; \\(m_t=-1\\) would give\n\\(n+1-2t=-(t-1)\\Rightarrow n=t\\), impossible because \\(t\\le n-2\\); and \\(m_t\\) is\nnever infinite.  Hence \\(\\sigma_t\\) is sunny.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd and \\(t_0=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) (the unique integer for which the slope\nin (18) would be \\(0\\)), set*  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{t_0}:\\;\\text{the line through }(1,t_0)\\text{ and }(t_0+1,n-t_0).\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nBoth defining points lie in \\(S\\) because\n\\(1+t_0\\le n\\) and \\((t_0+1)+(n-t_0)=n+1\\).  Its slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_{t_0}= \\frac{n-t_0-t_0}{t_0}= \\frac{-(1)}{t_0}\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\sigma_{t_0}\\) is also sunny.\n\nIn every case \\(\\sigma_t\\) contains exactly the two points listed above, both\nbelonging to \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  Starting from the family (17) we replace the\nfirst \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\) by the sunny lines\n\\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\).  The resulting set of \\(n\\) distinct lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\sigma_t\\;(t=1,\\dots ,k)\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\bigl\\{y=j\\;(j=k+1,\\dots ,n-2)\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(\\sigma_t\\)\u2019s) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\nlines.\n\n#### Lemma\u202f4  \n\\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le k\\).*  \nThe line \\(\\sigma_b\\) contains the point \\((1,b)\\).  If \\(a=1\\) we are done.\nAssume \\(a\\ge2\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\).  If the\ninequality is strict, then \\(a+b\\le n\\) and consequently \\(a+b=n+1\\) is false;\nthe only remaining possibility for a point with \\(b\\le k\\) and \\(a\\ge2\\) is\n\\(a+b=n+1\\).  In that situation the point lies on the diagonal line\n\\(x+y=n+1\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(k<b\\le n-2\\).*  \nThe horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\); thus the point is\ncovered.\n\n*Case 3: \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\).*  \nThese are handled exactly as in Lemma\u202f3: if \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le2\\); the\npoint \\((1,n-1)\\) lies on \\(x=1\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) lies on the diagonal\n\\(x+y=n+1\\).  If \\(b=n\\) then necessarily \\(a=1\\), and the point lies on the\ndiagonal as well.\n\nAll possibilities for \\((a,b)\\in S\\) have been exhausted, so every point of\n\\(S\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all required\nlattice points.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final conclusion  \n\nCorollary\u202f2 excludes \\(k\\ge n-1\\); Lemma\u202f3 gives a construction for \\(k=0\\);\nLemma\u202f4 provides constructions for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Hence the\nset of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
