ining points belong to \\(S\\) because \\((t+1)+(n-t)=n+1\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\sigma_t\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_t=\\frac{n-t-(t)}{t+1-1}= \\frac{n-2t}{t}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\ge1\\) we have \\(m_t\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore \\(\\sigma_t\\) is sunny.\nMoreover \\(\\sigma_t\\) contains exactly the two points \\((1,t)\\) and\n\\((t+1,n-t)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  Starting from the family (7) we replace the\nfirst \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\) by the sunny lines\n\\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\).  The resulting set of \\(n\\) distinct lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\sigma_t\\;(t=1,\\dots ,k)\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\bigl\\{y=j\\;(j=k+1,\\dots ,n-2)\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(\\sigma_t\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\nlines.\n\n#### Lemma 4  \n\\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le k\\).*  \nThe line \\(\\sigma_b\\) contains the point \\((1,b)\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) we are done.  \nIf \\(a\\ge2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  When the inequality is strict,\n\\(a+b\\le n\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) only when \\(a+b=n+1\\); otherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\) forces\n\\(a\\le n-b\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) is still on the diagonal because the\nonly lattice points of \\(S\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\) and abscissa\ndifferent from \\(1\\) are \\((t+1,n-b)\\) for \\(t=b\\), i.e. exactly the second\npoint of \\(\\sigma_b\\).  Hence every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le k\\) belongs either\nto \\(\\sigma_b\\) (if \\(a=1\\)) or to the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(k<b\\le n-2\\).*  \nThe horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\); thus the point is\ncovered.\n\n*Case 3: \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\).*  \nThese are handled exactly as in Lemma 3: if \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le2\\); the\npoint \\((1,n-1)\\) lies on \\(x=1\\) and the point \\((2,n-1)\\) satisfies\n\\(a+b=n+1\\) and lies on the diagonal; if \\(b=n\\) then necessarily\n\\(a=1\\) and the point lies on the diagonal.\n\nAll possibilities for \\((a,b)\\in S\\) have been covered, so every point of\n\\(S\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all required\nlattice points.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final conclusion  \n\nCorollary 2 excludes \\(k\\ge n-1\\); Lemma 3 gives a construction for \\(k=0\\);\nLemma 4 provides constructions for every \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Hence the set of\nadmissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n∎"
