).\n\n6. **Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)** \u2013  \n   Fix \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  Starting from the family (8) we replace the\n   first \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\) by the sunny lines\n   \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\).  The resulting set of \\(n\\) distinct lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\n   \\bigl\\{\\sigma_t\\;(t=1,\\dots ,k)\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\bigl\\{y=j\\;(j=k+1,\\dots ,n-2)\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\{x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   It contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   Lemma\u202f4 proves that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) still covers every point of \\(S\\):\n   * points with second coordinate \\(b\\le k\\) are covered either by the sunny\n     line \\(\\sigma_b\\) (which contains \\((1,b)\\)) or, if the abscissa is\n     \\(\\ge2\\), by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n   * points with \\(k<b\\le n-2\\) lie on the unchanged horizontal line \\(y=b\\);\n   * points with \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\) are covered exactly as in Lemma\u202f3 by the\n     vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the diagonal line.\n\n7. **Conclusion** \u2013 Corollary\u202f2 excludes \\(k\\ge n-1\\); Lemma\u202f3 gives a\n   construction for \\(k=0\\); Lemma\u202f4 provides constructions for all\n   \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of required lattice points  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the line \\(a+b=s\\) contains\n\\(s-1\\) points of \\(S\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or has\nslope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  All other lines are *sunny*.\n\n#### Lemma