            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of required lattice points  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the line \\(a+b=s\\) contains\n\\(s-1\\) points of \\(S\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or has\nslope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  All other lines are *sunny*.\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is sunny then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with rational slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) (if \\(m\\) is\nirrational the line contains no lattice point).  Let \\(m=\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) be in\nlowest terms, \\(q>0\\).  Any integer point on \\(\\ell\\) has the form  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_{0}+qt,\\;y_{0}+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nfor a particular solution \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\).  The conditions\n\\(1\\le x,y\\le n\\) give  \n\n\\[\n1-x_{0}\\le qt\\le n-x_{0},\\qquad 1-y_{0}\\le pt\\le n-y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nHence the admissible integers \\(t\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\nt\\in I:=\\Bigl[\\max\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{1-x_{0}}{q},\\frac{1-y_{0}}{p}\\Bigr),\n            \\min\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{n-x_{0}}{q},\\frac{n-y_{0}}{p}\\Bigr)\\Bigr]\\cap\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe length of the interval \\(I\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\min\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{n-1}{|q|},\\frac{n-1}{|p|}\\Bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the distance between the two endpoints of \\(I\\) is bounded by the\nsmaller of the two ranges \\(\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\) and \\(\\frac{n-1}{|p|}\\); adding one\naccounts for the integer endpoints.\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is sunny we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\) and \\((p,q)\\neq(0,\\pm1)\\).\nA short case analysis on the possible values of \\(|p|\\) and \\(|q|\\) shows that\nthe right–hand side of (4) never exceeds \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\):\n*If \\(|p|\\ge2\\) then \\(\\frac{n-1}{|p|}+1\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\);*\n*if \\(|q|\\ge2\\) the same bound follows;*\n*the remaining possibilities \\((|p|,|q|)=(1,1)\\) are excluded because they give\n\\(m=1\\) or \\(m=-1\\), which are non‑sunny slopes.*\n\nThus \\(|\\ell\\cap S|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 1′ (trivial bound)  \nIf a line is horizontal, vertical or of the form \\(x+y=c\\) then it meets at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); the number of such points is \\(n+1-b\\le n\\).  \nA vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); again at most \\(n\\) points.  \nFor a line \\(x+y=c\\) we have \\(c\\le n+1\\); the admissible points satisfy\n\\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(b=c-a\\), giving at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(S\\) and contain\n\\(s\\) sunny lines.  Using Lemma 1 for the sunny lines and Lemma 1′ for the\nremaining \\(n-s\\) lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S|\n   \\le s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-s)n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSolving (5) for \\(s\\) gives \\(s\\le n-2\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\le n-2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Corollary 2.*  No configuration can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  A covering with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nL_{n-1}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny.\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (i.e. \\(L_b\\)) contains the\n  point.\n\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n  – For \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(x=1\\) (\\(L_{n-1}\\)).  \n  – For \\(a=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line\n    \\(x+y=n+1\\) (\\(L_n\\)).\n\n* If \\(b=n\\) then necessarily \\(a=1\\); the point satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and lies\n  on \\(L_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) is covered. ∎\n\n\n\nHence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Sunny lines that will replace some horizontals  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\ge1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_t:\\text{ the line through }(1,t)\\text{ and }(t+1,n-t).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBoth defining points belong to \\(S\\) because \\((t+1)+(n-t)=n+1\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\sigma_t\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_t=\\frac{n-t-(t)}{t+1-1}= \\frac{n-2t}{t}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\ge1\\) we have \\(m_t\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore \\(\\sigma_t\\) is sunny.\nMoreover \\(\\sigma_t\\) contains exactly the two points \\((1,t)\\) and\n\\((t+1,n-t)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  Starting from the family (7) we replace the\nfirst \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\) by the sunny lines\n\\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\).  The resulting set of \\(n\\) distinct lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\sigma_t\\;(t=1,\\dots ,k)\\bigr