loor\\le 1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right\u2013hand side of (2) is at most \\(2\\).  \n   Therefore **any sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n\n4. **Impossibility of any sunny line.**  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least one sunny line.  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines; then \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\):  \n   - If the line is horizontal, all points of \\(S_n\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, so at most one lies on the line.  \n   - If the line is vertical, all points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, so at most one lies on the line.  \n   - If the line has equation \\(y=-x+c\\), then any point \\((a,b)\\) on it satisfies \\(a+b=c\\).  \n     Since \\(a+b\\) ranges from \\(2\\) up to \\(n+1\\) over \\(S_n\\), at most one value of \\(c\\) occurs, hence at most one point of \\(S_n\\) lies on such a line.\n\n   Consequently the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(n-k\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   By the bound proved in step\u202f3, each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\n   Altogether we obtain the inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)+2k \\;\\ge\\; |S_n| \\;=\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n+k \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   k \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right\u2013hand side exceeds \\(n\\), which is impossible because \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   Hence our assumption that a sunny line exists leads to a contradiction.\n\n   Therefore **every line in any admissib