at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Lemma 3 (line of slope \\(-1\\)).*  \n   A line \\(x+y=c\\) (with integer \\(c\\)) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\ge 1\\).  \n   The admissible values of \\(a\\) are exactly \\(a=1,\\dots ,c-1\\) while also requiring \\(c-a\\le n\\).  \n   Consequently the number of points is \\(\\min\\{c-1,\\,n+1-c\\}\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *Lemma 4 (sunny line).*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Write its equation in reduced rational form  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\; q\\ge 1,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(q\\ge 2\\) because the slope is not an integer (otherwise it would be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n   Suppose \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in S_n\\cap\\ell\\) with \\(x_1<x_2\\).  \n   Subtracting the two instances of (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y_2-y_1=\\frac{p}{q}(x_2-x_1).\n   \\]\n\n   Both sides are integers, hence \\(q\\mid (x_2-x_1)\\).  \n   Therefore the \\(x\\)-coordinates of distinct lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) differ by a multiple of \\(q\\).  \n   Since all points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), the distance between the smallest and the largest possible \\(x\\)-coordinate on \\(\\ell\\) is at most \\(n-1\\).  \n   Consequently the number of distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates that can appear on \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Big\\rfloor\\le 1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right–hand side of (2) is at most \\(2\\).  \n   Hence **any sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).**\n\n5. **Counting argument.**  \n   Assume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  \n   Split \\(\\mathcal L\\) into the \\(k\\) sunny lines and the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   By Lemmas 1–3 a non‑sunny line can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M_{\\text{ns}}:=\\max\\{\\,n,\\; \\big\\lfloor n/2\\big\\rfloor\\,\\}=n\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\) points.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   By Lemma 4 each sunny line covers at most \\(2\\) points, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\n   Because every point of \\(S_n\\) must lie on at least one line, we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\,n+2k\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying (3),\n\n   \\[\n   n^2 -kn +2k \\ge \\frac{n^2+n}{2}\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   \\frac{n^2}{2}+ \\Bigl(\\frac{n}{2}-k\\Bigr)n +2k \\ge 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines), inequality (4) can hold only when \\(n\\le 2\\).  \n   Our hypothesis \\(n\\ge 3\\) makes (4) impossible unless \\(k=0\\).\n\n   Therefore **any admissible configuration must have \\(k=0\\).**\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction of Step 2 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \n   The counting argument of Step 5 proves that no configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) can exist for \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
