integer \\(c\\)) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((a,c-a)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\ge 1\\).  \n   Consequently \\(|S_n\\cap\\{x+y=c\\}|=\\min\\{c-1,\\;n+1-c\\}\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *Lemma 4 (sunny line).*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Write its equation in reduced rational form  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\; q\\ge 1,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   where the slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) satisfies \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   Suppose \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in S_n\\cap\\ell\\) with \\(x_1<x_2\\).  \n   Subtracting the two instances of (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y_2-y_1=\\frac{p}{q}(x_2-x_1).\n   \\]\n\n   Both sides are integers, therefore \\(q\\mid (x_2-x_1)\\).  \n   Hence the \\(x\\)-coordinates of distinct lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) differ by a multiple of \\(q\\).  \n   Because all points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), the number of distinct admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates on \\(\\ell\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) we have \\(q\\ge 2\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Big\\rfloor\\le 1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **any sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n\n4. **Counting argument.**  \n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  \n   Split \\(\\mathcal L\\) into the \\(k\\) sunny lines and the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   By Lemmas 1–3 a non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Hence the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   By Lemma 4 each sunny line covers at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n   Because every point of \\(S_n\\) must lie on at least one line, we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n\\;+\\;k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (3) for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\le\\;\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).**\n\n5. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   Use the \\(k\\) distinct lines of slope \\(1\\)  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) the points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_i\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,a+i)\\quad\\text{with}\\quad 1\\le a\\le n-i .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly \\(n-i\\) points of \\(S_n\\); in particular each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny because its slope \\(1\\neq0,-1\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   For the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=j\\qquad(j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(x=j\\) contains the points \\((j,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\); thus it covers exactly \\(n+1-j\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and \\(b-a\\) is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-a\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{b-a}\\) (note that \\(b-a\\le k-1\\) because \\(a\\le k\\)).  \n   If \\(a>k\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is among the non‑sunny lines) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all of them distinct. Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument (4) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, while the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is attainable.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All admissible }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
