            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three\nforbidden directions: vertical \\((0,1)\\), horizontal \\((1,0)\\) and diagonal\n\\((1,-1)\\).  Hence a non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the lines  \n\n\\[\nx=\\text{const},\\qquad y=\\text{const},\\qquad x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{d_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies\n\\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in this range, hence lies on exactly one line of\n\\(\\mathcal D\\).  Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n#### 2.1 Choice of special points\nSelect \\(k\\) distinct points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nsuch that the sums  \n\n\\[\ns_i:=a_i+b_i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise different.  This is possible because the possible sums are the\n\\(n\\) integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).\n\n#### 2.2 Sunny lines through the special points\nFor each \\(i\\) draw a line \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) whose slope \\(\\alpha_i\\)\nsatisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(j\\neq i).\n\\]\n\nThe \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct sunny lines, each covering its designated point\n\\(P_i\\).\n\n#### 2.3 Non‑sunny diagonal lines covering the remaining points\nRemove from \\(\\mathcal D\\) the diagonals whose constants equal the chosen sums\n\\(s_i\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D'=\\{d_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\;c\\notin\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal D'|=n-k\\).  Every point of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinate sum is not\namong the \\(s_i\\) lies on exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal D'\\).\n\n#### 2.4 Obtaining exactly \\(n\\) lines\nIf the diagonal \\(d_2\\) (i.e. the line \\(x+y=2\\)) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D'\\),\nthe point \\((1,1)\\) is already covered.  If, however, \\(d_2\\) has been removed\n(because one of the chosen sums equals \\(2\\)), then \\((1,1)\\) would be\nuncovered.  In that situation we replace the missing diagonal by the vertical\nline  \n\n\\[\nv:\\;x=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal D'\\setminus\\{d_2\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{v\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-k-1)}_{\\text{remaining diagonals}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{1}_{v}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny lines}}\n\\;=\\;n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) is\ncovered: points whose sum is one of the \\(s_i\\) are covered by the corresponding\nsunny line \\(\\ell_i\\); all other points are covered by a diagonal in\n\\(\\mathcal D'\\), and the point \\((1,1)\\) (the only point with sum \\(2\\) that\nmight have been lost) is covered by the vertical line \\(v\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) a required configuration\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny\nlines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  Each sunny line has slope different from\n\\(-1\\); therefore it can intersect a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (which has slope\n\\(-1\\)) in at most one point.  There are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals\n\\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  Hence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be\ncovered by the \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\).\n\nBut \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n,\n\\]\n\nso the \\(n\\) sunny lines would have to cover more than \\(n\\) points on at least\none diagonal.  That would force two distinct points of the same diagonal to\nlie on the same sunny line, which is impossible because a line of slope\ndifferent from \\(-1\\) meets the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point.\nThus a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nAll integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realized, and \\(k=n\\) is\nimpossible.  Hence the set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
