a_i+b_i=s_i .\n\\]\n\nSuch a point exists because the equation \\(a+b=s_i\\) has the integer solutions\n\\((1,s_i-1),\\dots ,(s_i-1,1)\\) and all of them satisfy the required inequalities.\n\n#### 2.2 Sunny lines\nThrough each \\(P_i\\) draw a line \\(\\ell_i\\) whose slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(j\\neq i).\n\\]\n\nThis is always possible (choose, for instance, distinct rational slopes different\nfrom \\(0,-1\\)).  The \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct sunny lines and each contains its\ndesignated point \\(P_i\\).\n\n#### 2.3 Non‑sunny diagonal lines for the remaining points\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D'=\\{d_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\;c\\notin\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal D'|=n-k\\).  Every point of \\(T_n\\) whose sum is not among the\n\\(s_i\\) lies on exactly one diagonal belonging to \\(\\mathcal D'\\).\n\n#### 2.4 Adding a line for the point \\((1,1)\\) if necessary\nThe point \\((1,1)\\) has sum \\(2\\).  If \\(2\\notin\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\) then\n\\(d_2\\in\\mathcal D'\\) and \\((1,1)\\) is already covered.  If \\(2\\in\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\)\nthen \\(d_2\\) has been removed; in this case we add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nv:\\;x=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n#### 2.5 Final family of lines\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathcal D'\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}, &\\text{if }2\\notin\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\},\\\\[4pt]\n(\\mathcal D'\\setminus\\{d_{c_0}\\})\\cup\\{v\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\},\n&\\text{if }2\\in\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwhere \\(c_0\\) is any element of \\(\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\) (its removal merely\nkeeps the total count correct).  In both cases  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal F|=(n-k)+k=n,\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered:\n* points with sum \\(s_i\\) are on \\(\\ell_i\\);\n* points whose sum is not among the \\(s_i\\) are on a diagonal of \\(\\mathcal D'\\);\n* the point \\((1,1)\\) is on a diagonal or on \\(v\\) as required.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration satisfying\nthe problem statement exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny\nlines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nd_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on this diagonal are  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1),\n\\]\n\nhence exactly \\(n\\) points.  A sunny line has slope different from \\(-1\\); such\na line can intersect the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) in **at most one** point.  Consequently,\nto cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(d_{n+1}\\) we need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nand because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, every line must be used on a distinct\npoint of this diagonal.  No line remains to cover any point whose sum is\ndifferent from \\(n+1\\) (for example \\((1,1)\\)), contradicting the covering\nrequirement.  Therefore a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nAll integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realized, and \\(k=n\\) is\nimpossible.  Hence the set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
