)) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is\ncovered, and the required configuration exists for \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Non‑existence for \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nSuppose a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.  \nLet \\(R\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) be the set of sums whose diagonals are not\npresent among the \\(n\\) lines.  Because the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\n|R|=k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c\\in R\\) the diagonal \\(d_c\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(d_c\\); therefore\nthe \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points from the union of the\nremoved diagonals.  Consequently we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\sum_{c\\in R}(c-1).\n\\]\n\nSince every \\(c\\ge 2\\), the right‑hand side is at least \\(|R|=k\\), with equality\nonly when every \\(c=2\\).  But there is only one diagonal with \\(c=2\\).  Hence\nthe inequality cannot hold for \\(k\\ge 2\\).  This contradiction shows that no\nconfiguration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
