in R_k\\)) are covered\n  by that diagonal.\n\n* Every omitted diagonal \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\le k+1\\) contains the point\n  \\((1,1)\\).  Since \\((1,1)\\) lies on each \\(\\ell_i\\), all points of the\n  omitted diagonals are covered (the point \\((1,1)\\) itself is covered, and any\n  other point of such a diagonal coincides with \\((1,1)\\) only when \\(c=2\\);\n  for \\(c>2\\) the diagonal has more points, but each of those additional points\n  also lies on the retained diagonal \\(d_c\\) when \\(c\\notin R_k\\); when\n  \\(c\\in R_k\\) the only uncovered point is \\((1,1)\\), which is already covered\n  by the sunny lines).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k>n\\)\n\nThe problem requires exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Hence having more than\n\\(n\\) sunny lines would already exceed the allowed number of lines, which is\nimpossible.  Therefore no configuration exists for \\(k>n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore, for any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
