.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k}\\,x + \\bigl(2-\\tfrac{i}{k}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the point \\((1,1)\\) (since\n   \\(1\\cdot\\frac{i}{k}+2-\\frac{i}{k}=1\\)).  Moreover, for any diagonal\n   \\(d_c\\) the intersection point of \\(\\ell_i\\) with \\(d_c\\) is obtained by\n   solving  \n\n   \\[\n   x+\\Bigl(\\frac{i}{k}x+2-\\frac{i}{k}\\Bigr)=c\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   x = \\frac{k(c-2)}{k+i},\\qquad\n   y = c-x .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(c\\le k+1\\) for all omitted diagonals, the denominator \\(k+i\\) is\n   larger than the numerator \\(k(c-2)\\), guaranteeing that \\(0<x< c\\) and\n   \\(0<y< c\\).  Hence the intersection point lies inside the integer lattice\n   region of \\(d_c\\).  Since the coordinates are rational with denominator\n   \\(k+i\\), they are not necessarily integers; however, we do **not** need the\n   intersection to be an integer point.  What matters is that **every integer\n   point of an omitted diagonal is covered**.  To achieve this we use a\n   different idea.\n\n   **Final sunny\u2011line scheme.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = -x + (i+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(-1\\), so \\(\\ell_i\\) is *not* sunny \u2013 this does not work.\n\n   The difficulty above shows that a uniform family of sunny lines cannot be\n   forced to pass through all integer points of the omitted diagonals simultaneously.\n   Instead we employ a *point\u2011by\u2011point* assignment using Lemma\u202f1.\n\n   **Point\u2011by\u2011point assignment.**  \n   For each omitted diagonal \\(d_c\\) (\\(c\\in R_k\\)) list its integer points\n   \\(P_{c,1},\\dots ,P_{c,c-1}\\) as above.  Because \\(c-1\\le k\\), we can assign\n   each point \\(P_{c,i}\\) to a distinct sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) (the same index\n   \\(i\\) is used for all diagonals).  We now construct the sunny lines\n   explicitly so that they contain **all** the assigned points.\n\n   For a fixed \\(i\\) consider the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i=\\{P_{c,i}\\mid c\\in R_k,\\;c-1\\ge i\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(S_i\\) contains at most one point from each diagonal, and all its\n   points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates (the \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(i\\)).\n   Hence the points of \\(S_i\\) are collinear: they all lie on the vertical line\n   \\(x=i\\).  Unfortunately a vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and\n   therefore **not sunny**.\n\n   To obtain a sunny line we rotate the vertical line slightly.  Choose a\n   small rational \\(\\varepsilon_i\\neq0\\) such that \\(\\varepsilon_i\\) is not\n   \\(-1\\) and not infinite.  Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = \\varepsilon_i (x-i) + (i,\\,\\text{any }y\\text{-value}).\n   \\]\n\n   More concretely, let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = \\varepsilon_i x + b_i,\n   \\qquad\n   b_i = i - \\varepsilon_i i .\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,i)\\).  For each diagonal\n   \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\ge i+1\\) the point \\(P_{c,i}=(i,c-i)\\) lies on the line\n   \\(x=i\\); moving the line by the tiny slope \\(\\varepsilon_i\\) does **not**\n   change its intersection with the integer lattice point \\((i,c-i)\\) because\n   the point still satisfies \\(y = \\varepsilon_i i + b_i = i\\).  Thus each\n   \\(\\ell_i\\) contains **all** points \\((i,c-i)\\) with \\(c\\in R_k\\) and\n   \\(c\\ge i+1\\).  Since \\(\\varepsilon_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny,\n   and the \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct because they have different slopes\n   \\(\\varepsilon_i\\).\n\n   Consequently, for every omitted diagonal \\(d_c\\) all its integer points are\n   covered by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\); the retained diagonals\n   cover the remaining points.  The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\), and\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k>n\\).**  \n   The problem requires exactly \\