).  \nA short case analysis shows that the only possible rational slopes produced by such integer differences are \\(0\\) (when \\(\\Delta b=0\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (when \\(\\Delta a=0\\)) or \\(-1\\) (when \\(\\Delta a=-\\Delta b\\)).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, none of these slopes is allowed; therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\nBecause \\(|S_{n+1}|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\) and we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny (each sunny line contributes at most one new point, while a non‑sunny line may cover many points).  \nHence any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Turning horizontal (or vertical) lines into sunny lines  \n\nWe now show how to increase the number of sunny lines while preserving the covering property.\n\n*Lemma 3.1.*  \nLet \\(b\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\).  \nReplace the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in (4) by the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_b:\\; y-b=\\lambda (x-1),\\qquad \\lambda\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThen the new family still covers all points of \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe point \\((1,b)\\in S_{n+1}\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\) by construction.  \nFor any other point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); if \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point already lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\); if \\(a+b<n+1\\) then \\(a\\le n-b\\) and consequently \\(a\\ge 2\\) forces \\(b\\le n-2\\), so the point \\((a,b)\\) is also on the diagonal line because the diagonal contains **all** points with sum \\(n+1\\) and the horizontal line is no longer needed for them.  Hence every point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) remains covered. ∎\n\nBecause \\(\\lambda\\) can be chosen arbitrarily (avoiding the two forbidden slopes), the line \\(\\ell_b\\) is sunny and distinct from all other lines already present.\n\nApplying Lemma 3.1 to any collection of distinct horizontal lines \\(y=b_1,\\dots ,y=b_k\\) (with \\(k\\le n-1\\)) we obtain a family of \\(n\\) lines that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(k\\) newly created \\(\\ell_{b_i}\\) together with the remaining \\(n-1-k\\) horizontals and the diagonal).  \nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration exists.\n\n*Lemma 3.2.*  \nFor \\(k=n\\) we can also construct a suitable family.  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nReplace each of them by a sunny line through the point \\((i,1)\\) with a slope \\(\\mu_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (choose the \\(\\mu_i\\) all distinct).  \nAll points \\((a,1)\\) are still covered (they lie on the corresponding sunny line).  \nAny point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which we keep in the family.  \nConsequently we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom Lemma 3.1 we have constructions for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\); Lemma 3.2 supplies the case \\(k=n\\).  \nTogether with the necessary bound (5) we conclude that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the answer to the problem is:** for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration of \\(n\\) lines exists is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
