most one point, hence also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\n3. **Counting argument.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \n   Denote by  \n\n   * \\(h\\) – the number of horizontal lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n   * \\(k\\) – the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (so \\(0\\le k\\le n-h\\)).  \n\n   The cardinality of \\(S_{n+1}\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_{n+1}|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   **Points covered by the horizontal lines.**  \n   If the horizontal lines are \\(y=b_{1},\\dots ,y=b_{h}\\) (all distinct), they together cover  \n\n   \\[\n   H:=\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-b_{i})\\quad\\text{points}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(h\\) the sum \\(H\\) is maximised by choosing the smallest possible rows, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{b_{1},\\dots ,b_{h}\\}=\\{1,2,\\dots ,h\\},\n   \\]\n\n   giving  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{\\max }(h)=\\sum_{b=1}^{h}(n+1-b)=h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   **Points that must be covered by the remaining lines.**  \n   The uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=|S_{n+1}|-H\\;\\le\\;|S_{n+1}|-H_{\\max }(h). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Every non‑horizontal line (vertical, diagonal or sunny) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) (see §2).  \n   Hence the \\(n-h\\) remaining lines can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-h)\\,n \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   points. Consequently we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-h)n\\;\\ge\\;U\\;\\ge\\;|S_{n+1}|-H_{\\max }(h). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (1) and (3) into (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   n(n-h)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\Bigl[h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\\Bigr].\n   \\]\n\n   After simplification we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-h)^{2}\\;\\ge\\;0, \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   which is always true. Equality in (7) is necessary for the covering to be possible, because otherwise the left‑hand side would be strictly larger than the right‑hand side, contradicting (6).  \n\n   Equality in (7) forces equality throughout the chain (6). Therefore:\n\n   * The horizontal rows must be exactly the smallest possible ones, i.e.  \n     \\(\\{b_{1},\\dots ,b_{h}\\}=\\{1,2,\\dots ,h\\}\\).  \n   * The uncovered points must be exactly \\(U= n(n-h)\\).  \n   * Each of the remaining \\(n-h\\) lines must attain its maximal capacity of \\(n\\) points; consequently every non‑horizontal line must be either the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which indeed contains \\(n\\) points) or a sunny line that also contains \\(n\\) points.  \n\n   A sunny line can contain at most one point from each row, so to reach the bound \\(n\\) it would have to contain a point from **every** row \\(y=1,\\dots ,n\\). Such a line would have to pass through the points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\), which are precisely the points of the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). Hence any non‑horizontal line attaining the bound \\(n\\) is forced to be the diagonal; a sunny line cannot achieve the bound.\n\n   Consequently the only possibilities are  \n\n   1. **All lines are horizontal**: \\(h=n\\), \\(k=0\\).  \n   2. **\\(n-1\\) horizontal lines together with the diagonal**: \\(h=n-1\\), \\(k=0\\).\n\n   In both cases the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument shows that a covering family of exactly \\(n\\) lines cannot contain a sunny line; the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\). The explicit constructions above demonstrate that \\(k=0\\) is indeed realizable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer:** \\(\\boxed{k=0}\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
