ts of \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \n\n3. **Counting argument with horizontal lines.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \n   Denote  \n\n   * \\(h\\) – the number of horizontal lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n   * \\(k\\) – the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (so \\(0\\le k\\le n-h\\)).  \n\n   Write the horizontal rows used as \\(y=b_{1},\\dots ,y=b_{h}\\) with all \\(b_{i}\\) distinct and \\(1\\le b_{i}\\le n\\).  \n   The total number of points covered by the horizontal lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   H:=\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-b_{i}). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(h\\) the sum \\(H\\) is maximised when the chosen rows are the smallest possible ones, i.e. \\(\\{b_{1},\\dots ,b_{h}\\}=\\{1,2,\\dots ,h\\}\\). In that case  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{\\max }(h)=\\sum_{b=1}^{h}(n+1-b)=h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the number of points **still uncovered** after the horizontal lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=|S_{n+1}|-H\\;\\le\\;|S_{n+1}|-H_{\\max }(h). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(n-h\\) lines are all non‑horizontal; each of them can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) (Section 2). Therefore they can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-h)n \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   points. In order to cover all points we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-h)n\\;\\ge\\;U\\;\\ge\\;|S_{n+1}|-H_{\\max }(h). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (1) and (4) into (7) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n(n-h)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\Bigl[h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\\Bigr].\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying the right‑hand side:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-h(n+1)+\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n   =\\frac{(n-h)(n-h+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (7) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   n(n-h)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-h)(n-h+1)}{2}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(n-h\\ge 0\\), we may divide by \\(n-h\\) (the case \\(n-h=0\\) will be treated separately) and obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   2n\\;\\ge\\;n-h+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (n-h)^{2}\\le 0. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (9) forces \\((n-h)=0\\); otherwise the left‑hand side would be positive, contradicting (9).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   h=n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad h=n-1. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The case \\(h=n\\) means **all** lines are horizontal; the case \\(h=n-1\\) means there are exactly \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines and one non‑horizontal line.\n\n4. **Structure of the non‑horizontal line when \\(h=n-1\\).**  \n\n   *Uncovered points.*  \n   When the horizontal rows are the smallest possible ones, i.e. \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,h\\), the uncovered points are precisely those with \\(y\\ge h+1\\). Because \\(h=n-1\\), the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n+1}\\mid b=n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) we have \\(a+n\\le n+1\\) iff \\(a=1\\); thus the only points with \\(b=n\\) belonging to \\(S_{n+1}\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the \\(n\\) points lying on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). Hence the single non‑horizontal line must contain **all** these \\(n\\) points.\n\n   *Uniqueness of the diagonal.*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be any line (not horizontal) that contains a point from each of the rows \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) belonging to \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \n   In row \\(y=b\\) the only point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) is \\((n+1-b,b)\\). Therefore \\(\\ell\\) must pass through the \\(n\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   (n,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\dots ,(1,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Any two distinct points determine a unique line; since all these points are collinear, the line determined by any pair among them is the same. Computing the slope between \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n-1}{1-n}=-1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the line equation is \\(x+y=n+1\\). Hence the only non‑horizontal line that can cover the uncovered points is the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). This line is **not sunny** (its slope is \\(-1\\)).\n\n5. **Exclusion of vertical lines.**  \n   Suppose a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\). It can contain at most one point from each row, so at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n+1}\\). If a vertical line were used, the number of horizontal lines would be at most \\(n-1\\). By the argument of Step 3 we would then have \\(h\\le n-2\\), which would make \\((n-h)\\ge 2\\) and contradict inequality (9). Therefore **no vertical line can appear** in a covering family of exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n6. **Sunny lines cannot appear.**  \n   A sunny line is non‑horizontal, non‑vertical and has slope different from \\(-1\\). As shown in Step 2 it can contain at most one point from each row, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n+1}\\). To achieve the bound \\(n\\) it would need to contain a point from **every** row \\(y=1,\\dots ,n\\). As proved in Step 4, the only line that meets each row in a point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) is the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\). Hence a sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \n\n   If a sunny line were present, the total number of points covered by the \\(n\\) lines would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{\\max }(h)+(n-1)+(n-h-1)n < |S_{n+1}|,\n   \\]\n\n   contradicting the covering requirement. Therefore **\\(k=0\\)**; no sunny line can be part of a feasible family.\n\n7. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   *All‑horizontal construction:*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n+1}\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \n\n   *Horizontal + diagonal construction:*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1,\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The first \\(n-1\\) lines cover all points with \\(b\\le n-1\\); the remaining points are exactly those on the diagonal, which is covered by the last line.  \n\n   Both families contain \\(n\\) distinct lines and contain **no** sunny line, so they realise \\(k=0\\).\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument forces the number of horizontal lines to be \\(n\\) or \\(n-1\\); the latter forces the unique remaining line to be the diagonal, which is not sunny. Vertical lines and sunny lines cannot appear. Hence any feasible family of \\(n\\) lines contains **no** sunny line. The constructions above show that \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer:** \\(\\boxed{k=0}\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
