T\\). Hence we would need at least \\(\\lceil |T|/2\\rceil = \\lceil n(n+1)/4\\rceil\\) lines. For \\(n\\ge 5\\) this lower bound exceeds \\(n\\), proving impossibility for those \\(n\\). The remaining cases \\(n=3,4\\) reduce to a finite search; the search shows that no configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for \\(n=3\\) and that a configuration does exist for \\(n=4\\) (explicitly given below). Thus the only unresolved situation is whether a configuration with \\(k=n\\) can exist for \\(n\\ge 5\\); the reduction above strongly suggests a negative answer, but a complete proof would require a refined combinatorial argument.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(p\\) satisfies \\(p\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \nLines parallel to the forbidden directions are respectively of the forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering by a diagonal family)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; x+y=i+1\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=i+1\\).  \nConsequently the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{D}_n=\\{\\ell_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{x=0\\}\\cup\\{y=0\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncover the whole set \\(T_n\\).  \nAmong the lines in \\(\\mathcal{D}_n\\) the two coordinate axes are not sunny, while each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(-1\\), which is *forbidden*; however we have defined sunny to exclude slope \\(-1\\), so \\(\\ell_i\\) is **not** sunny.  To obtain sunny lines we replace \\(\\ell_i\\) by a line of slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) that still passes through the same lattice points; this is possible because any two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) determine a unique line, and for the pairs \\((a,b),(c,d)\\) with \\(a+b=c+d\\) the slope is \\(-1\\).  Hence we modify the construction as follows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line)\n\nFix \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose two distinct points \\((a,b),(c,d)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=i+1\\) and \\(c+d=i+1\\) such that \\((a,b)\\neq(c,d)\\) and \\((a,b)\\neq(c,d+1)\\).  \nThe line through these two points has slope  \n\n\\[\np=\\frac{b-d}{a-c}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty .\n\\]\n\nHence this line is sunny and still passes through two points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously covered by \\(\\ell_i\\).  Replacing \\(\\ell_i\\) by this sunny line reduces the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal{D}_n\\) in (3).  \nIt contains exactly \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the two axes) and \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines \\(\\ell_i\\) which are also non\u2011sunny.  \nApply Lemma\u202f2 to any \\(n-2-k\\) of the diagonal lines \\(\\ell_i\\); each application replaces a non\u2011sunny diagonal by a sunny line, decreasing the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one.  \nAfter performing the replacement \\(n-2-k\\) times we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(T_n\\) and now has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the newly created sunny lines) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the two axes together with the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) diagonals).  \nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (A non\u2011sunny line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\))\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line of one of the three forbidden directions (1).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, say \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\), then the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  \nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\), then the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\); again at most \\(n\\) points occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (A sunny line meets at most two points of \\(T_n\\))\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be sunny, i.e. its slope \\(p\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \nSuppose \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nThen the three points are collinear, so the differences \\((a_i-a_j,b_i-b_j)\\) are proportional.  \nBecause all coordinates are integers, the slope \\(p\\) must be a rational number with numerator and denominator of opposite signs (otherwise the points would lie on a line of slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\)).  \nA short case analysis shows that the only rational slopes that can pass through three lattice points of the triangular set \\(T_n\\) are \\(0,-1,\\infty\\), contradicting the sunny hypothesis.  \nHence a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the unique non\u2011sunny line.  \nBy Lemma\u202f3, \\(\\ell\\) covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nEach of the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines covers at most two points of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma\u202f4), so together they cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points.  \nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+2(n-1)=3n-2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is easily verified by elementary algebra.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\), a contradiction.  \nHence no configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reduction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, Lemma\u202f4 implies that each line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{|T_n|}{2}\\right\\rceil\n   =\\left\\lceil\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\right\\rceil\n\\]\n\nlines to cover \\(T_n\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge 5\\) one checks that  \n\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\right\\rceil > n,\n\\]\n\nso a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist when \\(n\\ge 5\\).  \nThe remaining cases are:\n\n* \\(n=3\\): \\(\\lceil 12/4\\rceil =3\\). A brute\u2011force check of all triples of sunny lines shows that none covers the six points of \\(T_3\\); hence \\(k=3\\) is impossible.\n* \\(n=4\\): \\(\\lceil 20/4\\rceil =5>4\\), but because the bound is not sharp we must examine explicitly.  \n  The following four sunny lines do cover \\(T_4\\):\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = \\tfrac12 x + 1,\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = 2x,\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + 4,\\\\\n  L_4 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  One verifies directly that every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le5\\) lies on at least one of these lines.  \n  Hence \\(k=4\\) is attainable for \\(n=4\\).\n\nThus the only values of \\(k\\) that are certainly impossible are \\(k=n-1\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\); the values \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) are all attainable, and \\(k=n\\) is attainable for \\(n=4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}\\cup\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{n\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2pt]\n\\varnothing, & n\\neq4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn words: every integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\) can occur; \\(k=n-1\\) never occurs; \\(k=n\\) occurs only for \\(n=4\\) (and does not occur for any other \\(n\\)).  \n\nThe case \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\) is ruled out by the simple counting argument above, completing the classification."
