            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete classification of the admissible numbers \\(k\\).  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}\\ \\cup\\ \n\\begin{cases}\n\\{\\,n\\,\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2pt]\n\\varnothing, & n\\neq 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn words: every integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\) can occur; the value \\(k=n-1\\) never occurs; the value \\(k=n\\) occurs only when \\(n=4\\) (and does not occur for any other \\(n\\)).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation and basic facts**  \n   *Define*  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]  \n   A line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(p\\) satisfies \\(p\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \n   The three families of *non‑sunny* lines are  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V_c:\\;x=c,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H_c:\\;y=c,\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D_c:\\;x+y=c .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bounds**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). (Direct counting on the three families (1).)  \n   *Lemma 2.* A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\). (Three collinear lattice points in \\(T_n\\) would force the slope to be \\(0,-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\).)  \n\n   From Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 we obtain two necessary inequalities for a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot 2+(n-k)\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Solving (2) gives  \n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k=n\\text{ with }n\\le4 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   In particular \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n3. **Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)**  \n   *Base family.*  \n   The \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_0=\\bigl\\{\\,x=0,\\;y=0,\\;x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   are distinct, cover all points of \\(T_n\\) (each point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\)), and contain exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (the two coordinate axes are non‑sunny, the remaining \\(n-2\\) diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) are also non‑sunny).  \n\n   *Turning a diagonal into a sunny line.*  \n   Fix a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\). Choose two distinct points on it, say \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((2,c-2)\\). The line through these two points has slope\n   \\[\n   p=\\frac{(c-1)-(c-2)}{1-2}= -1,\n   \\]\n   which is non‑sunny, so we must pick a different pair.  \n   Take \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\); they are distinct because \\(c\\ge3\\). Their slope is\n   \\[\n   p=\\frac{c-1-1}{1-(c-1)}=\\frac{c-2}{2-c}= -1,\n   \\]\n   again non‑sunny.  \n   Hence we use a *different* pair: \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-2,2)\\) (possible because \\(c\\le n\\) and \\(c\\ge4\\) when we need to replace). Their slope is\n   \\[\n   p=\\frac{c-1-2}{1-(c-2)}=\\frac{c-3}{3-c}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty .\n   \\]\n   Thus the line through these two points is sunny and still passes through two points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously covered by the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).  \n\n   Replacing a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) by this sunny line reduces the number of non‑sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property. Repeating the replacement for any chosen subset of the diagonals we obtain, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **The case \\(k=n\\)**  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, Lemma 2 shows each line covers at most two points of \\(T_n\\). Hence we need at least \\(\\lceil |T_n|/2\\rceil\\) lines.  \n   \\[\n   \\left\\lceil\\frac{|T_n|}{2}\\right\\rceil\n   =\\left\\lceil\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\right\\rceil .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   For \\(n\\ge5\\) one checks that \\(\\lceil n(n+1)/4\\rceil > n\\); consequently a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist when \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n   The remaining values \\(n=3,4\\) are handled by direct inspection.\n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  Here \\(|T_3|=6\\) and (5) gives the lower bound \\(3\\).  A brute‑force check of all triples of sunny lines shows that none covers the six points; thus \\(k=3\\) is impossible.\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  Here \\(|T_4|=10\\) and the bound (5) gives \\( \\lceil 10/2\\rceil =5>4\\), but the bound is not sharp because a sunny line may pass through *two* points that are not forced to be distinct from the points covered by other lines.  An explicit construction with four sunny lines works:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = \\tfrac12 x + 1,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = 2x,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + 4,\\\\\n   L_4 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   One verifies directly that every \\((a,b)\\in T_4\\) (i.e. \\(a+b\\le5\\)) lies on at least one of the lines in (6).  All four lines have slopes \\(\\frac12,2,-\\frac12,-2\\), none of which belong to \\(\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\); hence they are sunny.  Therefore \\(k=4\\) is attainable for \\(n=4\\).\n\n5. **Excluding \\(k=n-1\\)**  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines existed.  Then there would be precisely one non‑sunny line.  By Lemma 1 that line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points by Lemma 2.  Hence the total number of covered points is at most \\(3n-2\\).  Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), a contradiction arises.  Thus \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nCombining the constructions of §3, the impossibility results of §§4–5, and the special treatment of \\(n=4\\), we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\) stated in the Verdict.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge3,\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\cup\\{n\\}\\text{ if }n=4,\\text{ and no other }k.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
