\\) sunny lines is at most \\(3s\\).  \n\nThe remaining \\(n-s\\) lines are not sunny; each of them is parallel to at\nleast one of \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y,\\ell_d\\).  Such a line can contain arbitrarily\nmany points of \\(T_n\\), but it can contain **no** point of at least one of\nthe three families \\(A,B,C\\).  Consequently the \\(n-s\\) non‑sunny lines can\ncover at most \\(2(n-s)\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (they may miss the whole family\n\\(A\\) or \\(B\\) or \\(C\\)).  \n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, so  \n\n\\[\n3s+2(n-s)\\ge |T_n|= \\frac{(n+1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA short computation gives  \n\n\\[\n3s+2n-2s\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n* **Two non‑sunny lines**\n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;y=1 .\n  \\]\n\n* **For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) a sunny line**\n  \\[\n  \\ell_c:\\; \\text{the line through }(1,c)\\text{ and }(c,1).\n  \\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_c\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{1-c}{c-1}= -1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(\\ell_c\\) is **not** parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\) or \\(\\ell_y\\); it is also\ndifferent from the diagonal family \\(\\{x+y=\\text{const}\\}\\) because its\nslope is \\(-1\\) only when \\(c=2\\), and for \\(c=2\\) the line is \\(x+y=3\\),\nwhich is parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\) but we have excluded \\(c=2\\) from the\nsunny set.  Consequently each \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\) is sunny, and we\nhave exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_2\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\).  Set \\(c=a+b\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we\n  have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  If \\(c=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)=(n,1)\\) or \\((1,n)\\),\n  already covered by \\(\\ell_1\\) or \\(\\ell_2\\).  If \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) then\n  \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_c\\): indeed \\(\\ell_c\\) passes through\n  the two points \\((1,c)\\) and \\((c,1)\\); the equation of \\(\\ell_c\\) is  \n\n  \\[\n  y = -\\frac{c-2}{c-1}\\,x + c .\n  \\]\n\n  Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  y = -\\frac{c-2}{c-1}a + c = -\\frac{a+b-2}{a+b-1}a + (a+b)\n      = b,\n  \\]\n\n  because \\(c=a+b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_c\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\nThe construction uses exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, so the value\n\\(k=n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining all intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we start from the configuration\nabove (which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines) and replace \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny\nlines \\(\\ell_c\\) by the corresponding diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad (2\\le c\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal \\(d_c\\) contains **all** points of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates sum\nto \\(c\\); consequently every point that was previously covered by the\nreplaced sunny line \\(\\ell_c\\) remains covered.  The two axis‑parallel lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) are left unchanged, so points with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) stay\ncovered as before.  No new line is parallel to any of \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y,\\ell_d\\)\nexcept the diagonals \\(d_c\\), which are precisely the non‑sunny lines we are\nallowed to use.  After the replacement we have\n\n* the two fixed non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\);\n* \\(n-2-k\\) diagonals \\(d_c\\) (non‑sunny);\n* \\(k\\) remaining sunny lines \\(\\ell_c\\).\n\nHence the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\), the total number of\nlines is still \\(n\\), and the covering property is preserved.\n\n-------------------------------