            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous classification of all admissible values of \\(k\\). I was able to prove that any integer \\(k\\) with  \n\\[\n0\\le k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\ncan be realised, and that \\(k>\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) is impossible under a natural (but not fully proved) bound on the number of lattice points that a sunny line can contain. Thus I present a **partial solution**: the set of admissible \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\) and contains this whole interval, but I have not been able to close the gap between the necessary and sufficient conditions.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Its cardinality is  \n   \\[\n   |S_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (the “forbidden” directions) will be called *non‑sunny*.  \n   - A vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) contains the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); it therefore covers exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   - A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) contains the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); it also covers \\(n+1-c\\) points.  \n   - A diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le c-1\\); it covers \\(c-1\\) points.  \n\n   Using only non‑sunny lines one can cover all of \\(S_{n}\\) with exactly \\(n\\) lines (for instance, the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) already cover every point). Hence **\\(k=0\\) is always attainable**.\n\n3. **A bound for sunny lines.**  \n   A *sunny* line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Let a sunny line be written in reduced integer form as  \n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{p}{q}x + b,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;p\\neq 0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n   \\]\n   Intersecting this line with the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields a segment whose projection on the \\(x\\)-axis has length at most \\(n\\). Because the step in the \\(x\\)-direction between two consecutive lattice points on the line is \\(q\\), the line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/q\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\). Since \\(q\\ge 1\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\), we have \\(q\\ge 2\\) unless the line is vertical (which is non‑sunny). Consequently every sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\). In particular it contains **at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points**, and for the purpose of a simple necessary condition we may use the weaker estimate that a sunny line contains at most \\(\\frac{n}{2}+1\\) points.\n\n   If \\(k\\) sunny lines are used, they together cover at most  \n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl(\\frac{n}{2}+1\\Bigr)\n   \\]\n   points of \\(S_{n}\\). The remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines must therefore cover at least  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\Bigl(\\frac{n}{2}+1\\Bigr)\n   \\]\n   points. Since a non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\), we obtain the necessary inequality  \n   \\[\n   (n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\Bigl(\\frac{n}{2}+1\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n   Simplifying gives  \n   \\[\n   k\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Hence **\\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) is necessary**.\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n   - Choose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines of the form  \n     \\[\n     y = \\frac{p_i}{2}x + b_i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k),\n     \\]\n     where each \\(p_i\\) is an odd integer with \\(|p_i|\\le n\\) and the intercepts \\(b_i\\) are chosen so that the line passes through exactly two points of \\(S_{n}\\) and no two of the chosen sunny lines share a point of \\(S_{n}\\). This is possible because the set \\(S_{n}\\) contains many pairs of points whose connecting line has slope \\(\\frac{p}{2}\\) with odd \\(p\\neq -2\\); by a simple counting argument we can select \\(k\\) disjoint such pairs. Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover exactly \\(2k\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   - Remove those \\(2k\\) points. The remaining set \\(S'_{n}\\) has cardinality  \n     \\[\n     |S'_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k.\n     \\]\n\n   - Cover \\(S'_{n}\\) with \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines as follows.  \n     *First* use the vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,x_{v}\\) where \\(x_{v}\\) is the largest integer such that the column \\(x=x_{v}\\) still contains at least one uncovered point; each such vertical line removes an entire column of points, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points.  \n     *If* after using all possible vertical lines fewer than \\(n-k\\) lines have been employed, use horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots\\) in the same greedy fashion.  \n     *Finally*, if a few points are still uncovered, they all lie on a single diagonal of the form \\(x+y=c\\); a single diagonal (which is non‑sunn