+b\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}\\le\\frac{n}{1+|m|}\\le\\frac{n}{2},\n   \\]\n   because \\(|m|\\ge1\\) and \\(m\\neq-1\\).  \n   Since the \\(x\\)-coordinates of distinct lattice points on \\(\\ell\\) differ by at\n   least \\(1\\), the number of such points is at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(q\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Two distinct lattice points on \\(\\ell\\) differ in their \\(x\\)-coordinates by a\n   multiple of \\(q\\).  Because all points of \\(S_{n}\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\),\n   the number of admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates is at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/q\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\).  \n   Moreover, when \\(q\\ge2\\) the line cannot contain the point \\((1,1)\\) (otherwise\n   its slope would be \\(-1\\)), so at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points belong to\n   \\(S_{n}\\).  ∎\n\n   Consequently, if a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines, those lines cover at\n   most \\(k\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n3. **Necessary condition.**  \n\n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them can contain at most\n   \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\) (a vertical, horizontal or diagonal line meets at most\n   \\(n\\) points of the triangle).  Therefore\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|S_{n}|-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Rearranging gives\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Hence **any feasible configuration must satisfy** \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n4. **A construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).**  \n\n   Fix such a \\(k\\).  We shall exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines that together cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   *Step 4.1 – Sunny lines covering exactly two points each.*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x + (2i-2) .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus\n   each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points\n   \\[\n   P_{2i-1}=(2i-1,\\,2i-1),\\qquad\n   P_{2i}   =(2i,\\,2i) .\n   \\]\n   Both points belong to \\(S_{n}\\) because\n   \\((2i-1)+(2i-1)=4i-2\\le n+1\\) and\n   \\((2i)+(2i)=4i\\le n+1\\) for all \\(i\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n   The sets \\(\\{P_{2i-1},P_{2i}\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, so the \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines together cover exactly the \\(2k\\) points\n   \\[\n   T:=\\{(1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(2k,2k)\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4.2 – Removing the covered points.*  \n   Let\n   \\[\n   S'_n:=S_{n}\\setminus T .\n   \\]\n   By (1) and (4)\n   \\[\n   |S'_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2k . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4.3 – Covering the remainder with \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Consider the vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\,x=2,\\,\\dots ,\\,x=n .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   For each column \\(x=c\\) the points of \\(S'_n\\) that lie in that column are\n   exactly the points \\((c,b)\\) with\n   \\[\n   1\\le b\\le n+1-c,\\qquad (c,b)\\neq(c,c).\n   \\]\n   Hence every column contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S'_n\\), and the column\n   \\(c=2i\\) (for \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)) is missing the point \\((2i,2i)\\) that was\n   already covered by a sunny line.  Consequently, after using the vertical\n   lines for **all columns except the \\(k\\) columns**\n   \\[\n   C:=\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   we have employed exactly \\(n-k\\) vertical lines and have covered every point\n   of \\(S'_n\\) **except possibly those lying in the columns of \\(C\\)**.\n\n   For a fixed \\(c\\in C\\) the uncovered points are\n   \\[\n   \\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c,\\;b\\neq c\\}.\n   \\]\n   All these points lie on the diagonal line\n   \\[\n   d_c:\\; x+y = c+(n+1-c)=n+1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   The line \\(