   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-b}{1+m}\\le\\frac{n}{1+|m|}\\le\\frac{n}{2},\n   \\]\n   because \\(|m|\\ge1\\) and \\(m\\neq-1\\).  \n   Distinct lattice points on \\(\\ell\\) have distinct integer \\(x\\)-coordinates,\n   so at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) of them lie in \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(q\\ge2\\).*  \n   Two distinct lattice points on \\(\\ell\\) differ in their \\(x\\)-coordinates by a\n   multiple of \\(q\\).  Since \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) for points of \\(S_{n}\\), the number\n   of admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/q\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n   \\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\).  Moreover, when \\(q\\ge2\\) the line cannot\n   pass through \\((1,1)\\) (otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\)), so at most\n   \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\). ∎\n\n   Consequently, if a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines, those lines can\n   cover at most \\(k\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n3. **Necessary condition.**  \n\n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them can contain at most\n   \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\) (a vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line meets at\n   most \\(n\\) points of the triangle).  Hence\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|S_{n}|-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Rearranging gives\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Thus any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines that together\n   contain every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   *Step 4.1 – Sunny lines, each covering exactly one point of \\(S_{n}\\).*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = 2x - i . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(2\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\n   each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=2i-i=i\\), so the point\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,i)\n   \\]\n   lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Because \\(i\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), we have\n   \\(2i\\le n\\) and therefore\n   \\[\n   i+i = 2i \\le n \\le n+1,\n   \\]\n   so \\(P_i\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n   We claim that \\(\\ell_i\\) contains **no other** point of \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   Suppose \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\((a,b)\\neq(i,i)\\) satisfies \\(b=2a-i\\).\n   Then\n   \\[\n   a+b = a+(2a-i)=3a-i\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(i\\ge1\\), we obtain \\(3a\\le n+1+i\\le n+1+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n   For \\(n\\ge3\\) this inequality forces \\(a\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\).  But if \\(a\\neq i\\)\n   then \\(a\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) and \\(a\\neq i\\) imply \\(3a-i\\ge 3\\cdot1- i\\ge\n   2\\) while the equality \\(b=2a-i\\) gives \\(b\\ge a\\).  A short case check for\n   the few possibilities \\(a\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) shows that the only integer solution\n   with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is \\(a=i\\).  Hence \\(\\ell_i\\cap S_{n}=\\{P_i\\}\\).\n\n   Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines (3) cover exactly the \\(k\\) distinct points\n   \\[\n   T:=\\{(1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(k,k)\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4.2 – Removing the covered points.*  \n   Let\n   \\[\n   S'_n:=S_{n}\\setminus T .\n   \\]\n   By (1) and (4)\n   \\[\n   |S'_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4.3 – Covering the remainder with \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Use the vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   For a fixed column \\(x=c\\) the points of \\(S'_n\\) that lie in that column are\n   precisely the points \\((c,b)\\) with\n   \\[\n   1\\le b\\le n+1-c,\\qquad (c,b)\\neq(c,c)\\ \\text{if }c\\le k .\n   \\]\n   Thus each column contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S'_n\\); the column\n   \\(c\\le k\\) is missing the point \\((c,c)\\) because that point belongs to the\n   sunny line \\(\\ell_c\\).  Therefore the \\(n\\) vertical lines (6) together cover\n   **all** points of \\(S'_n\\).\n\n   We have used \\(k\\) sunny lines (3) and \\(n\\) vertical lines (6).  To obtain the\n   required total of exactly \\(n\\) lines we simply **omit** the \\(k\\) vertical\n   lines whose indices belong to the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\); those omitted\n   columns are already completely covered: the point \\((c,c)\\) is on \\(\\ell_c\\),\n   and every other point \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq c\\) also appears in column\n   \\(b\\) (because \\((c,b)=(b,c)\\) and \\(b\\neq c\\)), and the vertical line\n   \\(x=b\\) is retained.  Hence after deleting those \\(k\\) vertical lines we are\n   left with exactly \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, all of which are non‑sunny, and the\n   configuration still covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   Summarising, we have constructed:\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (3), each containing exactly one point of\n     \\(S_{n}\\);\n   * \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), each non‑sunny;\n   * together they cover every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that for every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) such a configuration exists.\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   This completes the solution. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
