ion must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n   We shall exhibit exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines that together contain every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   *Step 4.1 – Sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = 2x - i . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\)\n   is sunny.  Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives the point\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,i)\\in\\ell_i .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(i\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), we have \\(2i\\le n\\) and thus\n   \\(i+i=2i\\le n\\le n+1\\); consequently \\(P_i\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n   **Claim.**  \\(\\ell_i\\cap S_{n}=\\{P_i\\}\\).  \n   Suppose \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(b=2a-i\\) and \\((a,b)\\neq(i,i)\\).\n   Then\n   \\[\n   a+b = a+(2a-i)=3a-i\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(i\\ge1\\), we obtain \\(3a\\le n+1+i\\le n+1+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\),\n   which forces \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  Because \\(a\\neq i\\) and both\n   \\(a,i\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), we have \\(3a-i\\ge 3\\cdot1- \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\ge2\\),\n   contradicting the inequality \\(3a-i\\le n+1\\) together with the integrality\n   constraints unless \\(a=i\\).  Hence no other lattice point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on\n   \\(\\ell_i\\).  ∎\n\n   Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines (3) cover exactly the diagonal points\n   \\[\n   T:=\\{(1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(k,k)\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4.2 – Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Consider the vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x = k+1,\\;x = k+2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x = n . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   There are \\(n-k\\) of them, and each is non‑sunny (parallel to the\n   \\(y\\)-axis).\n\n   *Step 4.3 – Verification of full coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\le k\\)* then either \\(a=b\\) (the point belongs to \\(T\\) and is on\n   \\(\\ell_a\\)), or \\(a\\neq b\\).  In the latter case \\(b\\ge a+1\\); consequently\n   \\(b\\ge k+1\\) because \\(a\\le k\\).  Hence the vertical line \\(x=b\\) from (5)\n   passes through \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\ge k+1\\)* then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (5) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines\n   \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{x=k+1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\).  The total number of\n   lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), exactly as required, and precisely \\(k\\) of them are\n   sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that for every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) such a configuration exists.\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   This completes the proof. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
