meets \\(S_{n}\\) only at\n   \\(P_i\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfy \\(b=2a-i\\).  From \\(b\\ge1\\) we obtain\n   \\(a\\ge\\frac{i+1}{2}\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes\n   \\[\n   a+(2a-i)=3a-i\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1+i}{3}.\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   \\frac{i+1}{2}\\le a\\le\\frac{n+1+i}{3}.\n   \\]\n   The length of this interval is\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n+1+i}{3}-\\frac{i+1}{2}\n   =\\frac{2n- i-1}{6}<1,\n   \\]\n   because \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).  An interval of length \\(<1\\) can contain at\n   most one integer, and that integer is forced to be \\(a=i\\).  Consequently\n   \\(b=2i-i=i\\), i.e. \\((a,b)=P_i\\). ∎\n\n   Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover precisely the diagonal points\n   \\[\n   D:=\\{(1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(k,k)\\}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   We use two families of axis‑parallel lines:\n\n   - vertical lines\n     \\[\n     x = k+1,\\;x = k+2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x = n ; \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n   - horizontal lines\n     \\[\n     y = 1,\\;y = 2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y = k . \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   The vertical family contains \\(n-k\\) lines, the horizontal family contains\n   \\(k\\) lines; together they give exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, as required.\n\n   *Verification of full coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n   - **If \\(a\\ge k+1\\)**, then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (4) passes through\n     \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   - **If \\(a\\le k\\)**, then either \\(a=b\\) (the point belongs to the diagonal\n     set \\(D\\) and is on \\(\\ell_a\\)), or \\(a\\neq b\\).  \n     In the latter case \\(b\\ge a+1\\).  Because \\(a\\le k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n     we have\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-k .\n     \\]\n     Since \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), we have \\(n+1-k\\ge k+1\\); consequently\n     \\(b\\) lies in the interval \\([k+1,\\,n]\\) or in \\([1,\\,k]\\).\n     If \\(b\\le k\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) from (5) contains the point.\n     If \\(b\\ge k+1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=b\\) from (4) contains the point.\n     In both sub‑cases the point is covered.\n\n   Hence every element of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines\n   \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{x=k+1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\cup\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\}\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;(\\text{non‑sunny})=n,\n   \\]\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  This completes the construction.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that for every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) such a family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   This finishes the proof. ∎"
