) on \\(\\ell\\) we have\n   \\[\n   a+b=a+ma+b_{0}=(1+m)a+b_{0}\\le n+1,\n   \\]\n   where \\(b_{0}=b\\) is the integer intercept.\n   Because \\(|m|\\ge1\\) and \\(m\\neq-1\\), we obtain\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-b_{0}}{1+|m|}\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Distinct points on \\(\\ell\\) have distinct integer \\(x\\)-coordinates, so at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) of them lie in \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(q\\ge2\\).*  \n   If two lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) have abscissae \\(a_{1},a_{2}\\), then\n   \\(a_{1}-a_{2}\\) is a multiple of \\(q\\); consequently the set of admissible\n   abscissae in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) has cardinality at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/q\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\).\n   Moreover, because the slope is not \\(-1\\), the line cannot pass through the\n   point \\((1,1)\\); hence at most one of the admissible abscissae can give a\n   point that also satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Therefore the total number of\n   points of \\(S_{n}\\) on \\(\\ell\\) does not exceed \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   In both cases the bound holds. ∎\n\n   Consequently, if a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines, those lines can\n   cover at most \\(k\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n3. **Necessary condition.**  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them can meet at most \\(n\\)\n   points of \\(S_{n}\\) (a vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line cuts the\n   triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points).  Hence\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|S_{n}|-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Rearranging gives\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Thus any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n   We shall exhibit exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines that together contain every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = 2x - i . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is\n   sunny.  Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives the lattice point\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,i)\\in\\ell_i .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(i\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), we have \\(2i\\le n\\) and hence\n   \\(P_i\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n   **Lemma 2.**  For every \\(i\\) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) meets \\(S_{n}\\) only at\n   \\(P_i\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfy \\(b=2a-i\\).  From \\(b\\ge1\\) we obtain\n   \\(a\\ge\\frac{i+1}{2}\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes\n   \\[\n   a+(2a-i)=3a-i\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1+i}{3}.\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   \\frac{i+1}{2}\\le a\\le\\frac{n+1+i}{3}.\n   \\]\n   The length of this interval is\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n+1+i}{3}-\\frac{i+1}{2}\n   =\\frac{2n- i-1}{6}<1,\n   \\]\n   because \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).  An interval of length \\(<1\\) can contain at\n   most one integer, and that integer is forced to be \\(a=i\\).  Consequently\n   \\(b=2i-i=i\\), i.e. \\((a,b)=P_i\\). ∎\n\n   Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover precisely the diagonal points\n   \\[\n   D:=\\{(1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(k,k)\\}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   We use two families of axis‑parallel lines:\n\n   - vertical lines\n     \\[\n     x = k+1,\\;x = k+2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x = n ; \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n   - horizontal lines\n     \\[\n     y = 1,\\;y = 2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y = k . \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   The vertical family contains \\(n-k\\) lines, the horizontal family contains\n   \\(k\\) lines; together they give exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, as required.\n\n   *Verification of full coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n   - **If \\(a\\ge k+1\\)**, then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (4) passes through\n     \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   - **If \\(a\\le k\\)**, then either \\(a=b\\) (the point belongs to the diagonal\n     set \\(D\\) and is on \\(\\ell_a\\)), or \\(a\\neq b\\).  \n     In the latter case \\(b\\ge a+1\\).  Because \\(a\\le k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n     we have\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-k .\n     \\]\n     Since \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), we have \\(n+1-k\\ge k+1\\); consequently\n     \\(b\\) lies in the interval \\([k+1,\\,n]\\) or in \\([1,\\,k]\\).\n     If \\(b\\le k\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) from (5) contains the point.\n     If \\(b\\ge k+1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=b\\) from (4) contains the point.\n     In both sub‑cases the point is covered.\n\n   Hence every element of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines\n   \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{x=k+1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\cup\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\}\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;(\\text{non‑sunny})=n,\n   \\]\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  This completes the construction.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that for every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) such a family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   This finishes the proof. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
