 points on \\(\\ell\\) have distinct integer abscissae, so at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) of them lie in \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(q\\ge2\\).*  \n   If \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in S_{n}\\cap\\ell\\) then\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b_{1}-b_{2}}{a_{1}-a_{2}}=\\frac{p}{q}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   a_{1}\\equiv a_{2}\\pmod q .\n   \\]\n   Hence the admissible abscissae form an arithmetic progression with step\n   \\(q\\).  Inside the interval \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there are at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/q\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) such\n   abscissae.\n\n   Moreover, because the slope is not \\(-1\\), the line cannot contain two\n   points whose sum of coordinates differs by \\(0\\) (otherwise the slope would be\n   \\(-1\\)).  Consequently at most one of the admissible abscissae can give a\n   point satisfying the additional condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Therefore the\n   total number of points of \\(S_{n}\\) on \\(\\ell\\) is at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   Both cases give the claimed bound. ∎\n\n3. **Necessary condition.**  \n   Let a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   By Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most\n   \\(k\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n   Each non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) meets the\n   triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points, so the \\(n-k\\) such lines can\n   cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.  Hence\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|S_{n}|-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Rearranging yields\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Thus any feasible configuration must satisfy (1).\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n   We exhibit exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines\n   covering all points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = 2x - i . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each\n   \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives the lattice point\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,i)\\in\\ell_i .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(i\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), we have \\(2i\\le n\\) and thus\n   \\(P_i\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n   **Lemma 2.**  For every \\(i\\) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) meets \\(S_{n}\\) only at\n   \\(P_i\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfy \\(b=2a-i\\).\n   From \\(b\\ge1\\) we obtain \\(a\\ge\\frac{i+1}{2}\\).\n   The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes\n   \\[\n   a+(2a-i)=3a-i\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1+i}{3}.\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   \\frac{i+1}{2}\\le a\\le\\frac{n+1+i}{3}.\n   \\]\n   The length of this interval is\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n+1+i}{3}-\\frac{i+1}{2}\n   =\\frac{2n-i-1}{6}<1,\n   \\]\n   because \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).  An interval of length \\(<1\\) can contain\n   at most one integer, and that integer must be \\(a=i\\).  Consequently\n   \\(b=2i-i=i\\), i.e. \\((a,b)=P_i\\). ∎\n\n   Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover precisely the diagonal points\n   \\[\n   D:=\\{(1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(k,k)\\}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   We use two families:\n\n   - **Vertical lines**\n     \\[\n     V_j:\\; x = k+j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2k). \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n     There are \\(n-2k\\) of them; each is vertical, hence non‑sunny.\n\n   - **Horizontal lines**\n     \\[\n     H_j:\\; y = j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k). \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n     There are \\(k\\) of them; each is horizontal, hence non‑sunny.\n\n   The total number of non‑sunny lines is \\((n-2k)+k=n-k\\), as required.\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the\n   constructed lines.*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n   1. **If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) and \\(a\\le n-k\\).**  \n      Then the vertical line \\(V_{a-k}\\) (from (4)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   2. **If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).**  \n      From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k\\).  \n      Hence the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\) (from (5)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   3. **If \\(a\\le k\\).**  \n      - When \\(b\\le k\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\).\n      - When \\(b\\ge k+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\); moreover\n        \\(b\\ge k+1\\) and \\(b\\le n-k\\) because \\(a\\le k\\) and\n        \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) imply \\(n+1-a\\ge n-k+1\\).  \n        Hence \\(b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) and the vertical\n        line \\(V_{b-k}\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   4. **If \\(a=b\\le k\\)** the point is one of the diagonal points \\(P_a\\) and\n      lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\n   In every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by at least one\n   of the lines \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\cup\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{\\,n-2k}\\cup\\{H_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-2k)+k\\;(\\text{non‑sunny})=n,\n   \\]\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  This completes the construction.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that for every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) such a family of \\(n\\) lines\n   exists.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or