   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For the point \\((a,b)=(a,i)\\) we have\n\n   \\[\n   i = a + (n+1-2i)\n   \\iff a = 2i - (n+1) + i = 3i - (n+1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a>v=n-k\\) and \\(i\\le k\\), inequality (5) is indeed satisfied:\n   from \\(a>n-k\\) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   a \\ge n-k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(i=b\\le k\\) we compute\n\n   \\[\n   3i-(n+1)\\ge 3\\cdot 1-(n+1)=2-n\\ge n-k+1\n   \\]\n\n   (the last inequality holds because \\(k\\le n\\)).  Consequently the\n   integer \\(a\\) defined by (5) lies in the interval \\([n-k+1,\\,n]\\),\n   i.e. \\(a>v\\).  Hence the point \\((a,i)\\) satisfies (4) and therefore\n   belongs to the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered either by a vertical line\n   (Case\u202fA) or by a sunny line (Case\u202fB).\n\n4. **Extremal values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Equation (1) gives \\(v=n\\); we obtain the \\(n\\) vertical\n   lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non\u2011sunny and, by Case\u202fA, cover\n   every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  Then \\(v=0\\) and the construction consists solely of the\n   \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n)\\).  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we\n   have \\(a\\ge 1\\); consequently \\(b\\le n\\) and we fall into Case\u202fB,\n   which shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_b\\).\n\n   *Intermediate \\(k\\).*  For any \\(0<k<n\\) the construction described in\n   steps\u202f2\u20133 works, giving exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) vertical\n   lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   We have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set\n   of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and\n   covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  The\n   obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no larger value of \\(k\\) is\n   possible.  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]"
