                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that **every** non‑negative integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncan occur as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds in two parts.\n\n1. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n   – First we cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) by a family \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) of at most \\(n\\) lines that are **not** sunny (i.e. each line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   – Then, if we need fewer than \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines, we replace a suitable number of them by distinct sunny lines, each of which is chosen to pass through a lattice point that is already covered. Because the point is already on a line, adding a new sunny line does not destroy the covering property, and the total number of lines stays \\(n\\).\n\n2. **Verification that the construction works for every \\(k\\).**  \n   – We show that the triangle \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points on each of the three families of parallel lines (horizontal, vertical, and diagonal). Consequently we can select a set of at most \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines that covers the whole triangle.  \n   – By deleting any \\(t\\) of those non‑sunny lines (with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\)) and inserting \\(t\\) distinct sunny lines through already‑covered points, we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) lines containing precisely \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is that every point of \\(T_n\\) must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) given lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering by non‑sunny lines\n\nConsider the three families of lines that are *not* sunny:\n\n* horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\));\n* vertical lines \\(x=c\\);\n* diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=c\\).  \nThere are \\(n\\) such diagonal lines, namely \\(d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\).\n\nSimilarly, for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nh_c:\\;y=c\n\\]\n\ncontains the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are \\(n\\) horizontal lines.  \nAnalogously, the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nv_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(1\\le c\\le n)\n\\]\n\nalso number \\(n\\).\n\nObserve that the set \\(\\{d_2,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\\) already covers **all** points of \\(T_n\\): each \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), hence lies on the diagonal line \\(d_c\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}:=\\{d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and satisfies the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Introducing sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nt:=n-k\\qquad (0\\le t\\le n).\n\\]\n\nWe shall replace \\(t\\) of the diagonal lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) by \\(t\\) distinct sunny lines, thereby obtaining a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of exactly \\(n\\) lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\nChoose any \\(t\\) distinct points of \\(T_n\\); for definiteness take  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1)\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,t .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(P_i\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(d_{i+1}\\) (because \\(i+1=i+1\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\) pick a line \\(\\ell_i\\) that\n\n* passes through \\(P_i\\);\n* is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nSuch a line exists because through any point there are infinitely many directions, and only three of them are forbidden. Moreover we can choose the \\(\\ell_i\\) so that they are pairwise distinct and also distinct from the remaining diagonal lines (e.g. give them distinct slopes).\n\nNow define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_{0}\\setminus\\{d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{t+1}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{t}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(k=n-t\\) diagonal (hence non‑sunny) lines and \\(t\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\); therefore it has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause each \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the point \\(P_i\\) which already belongs to the removed diagonal line \\(d_{i+1}\\), the point \\(P_i\\) remains covered. All other points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered by the unchanged diagonal lines that remain in \\(\\mathcal L\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both conditions of the problem, with exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Exhaustiveness\n\nThe construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConversely, any admissible family contains at most \\(n\\) lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\), and it is clearly non‑negative. Hence the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) not exceeding \\(n\\) are attainable.**"
