eal number \\(\\alpha_{c}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{c}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(\\alpha_{c}\\) is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor undefined (vertical), nor \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nDefine \\(\\ell_{c}\\) to be the line through \\(P_{c}\\) with slope \\(\\alpha_{c}\\).  \nBy construction \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is sunny, passes through \\(P_{c}\\), and for distinct \\(c\\) we can pick distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_{c}\\); hence the \\(\\ell_{c}\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from the lines in \\(S\\).\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=S\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_{c}\\mid d_{c}\\in R\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) non‑sunny lines (the members of \\(S\\)) and \\(n-k\\) sunny lines (the \\(\\ell_{c}\\)), altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\n*If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(S\\)*, it is already covered, because the lines of \\(S\\) are unchanged from \\(\\mathcal D\\).\n\n*If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on a line \\(d_{c}\\in R\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).  \nIn particular the point \\(P_{c}=(1,c-1)\\) also lies on \\(d_{c}\\).  \nSince we have replaced \\(d_{c}\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{c}\\) which passes through the same point \\(P_{c}\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) is still covered: it lies on the line \\(\\ell_{c}\\) because \\(\\ell_{c}\\) contains the whole diagonal \\(d_{c}\\)?  \n\n*Correction*: the line \\(\\ell_{c}\\) does **not** contain the whole diagonal; it only contains the single point \\(P_{c}\\).  \nHence we must argue differently.\n\nObserve that every point of the diagonal \\(d_{c}\\) has the form \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\).  \nAmong these points the one with \\(a=1\\) is precisely \\(P_{c}\\).  \nThus the only point of \\(d_{c}\\) that might become uncovered after removing \\(d_{c}\\