ave different slopes.\n\n   Now replace the diagonal \\(d_{c}\\) by the **pair** \\(\\{\\ell^{(1)}_{c},\\ell^{(2)}_{c}\\}\\).  \n   The points of \\(S_{c}\\) that are not \\((1,c-1)\\) or \\((t,c-t)\\) remain covered, because each such point \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(a\\neq 1,t\\) also lies on the diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   d_{a+1}:\\;x+y=a+1,\n   \\]\n\n   and \\(d_{a+1}\\) is still present in the family (we have removed only \\(d_{c}\\)).  \n   Hence after the replacement every point of the required domain is still covered.\n\n3. **Inductive construction of any number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the base family \\(\\mathcal D\\) (which contains \\(0\\) sunny lines), we apply the replacement described in step\u202f2 to a diagonal \\(d_{c}\\) **once**.  \n   This operation removes one non\u2011sunny line and adds **two** sunny lines, thereby increasing the number of sunny lines by \\(2\\) while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) (the increase of \\(+2\\) is compensated by the fact that we have removed one line, so the net change is \\(+1\\)).  \n\n   Repeating the operation on distinct diagonals we can increase the number of sunny lines by one at each step.  \n   Because there are \\(n\\) diagonals, we can perform the operation at most \\(n\\) times, obtaining families with \\(0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   The only subtle case is when we wish to replace the diagonal \\(d_{2}\\) (the one with only the single point \\((1,1)\\)).  \n   For this diagonal we simply replace it by a single sunny line passing through \\((1,1)\\) with any admissible slope (e.g. slope \\(2\\)).  \n   This increases the sunny count by \\(1\\) while preserving the covering, because \\((1,1)\\) is the only point that needed to be covered on \\(d_{2}\\).\n\n4. **Verification of the final family.**  \n   *The number of lines.*  \n   At each replacement we delete one diagonal and insert either one (for \\(d_{2}\\)) or two sunny lines; the net effect is always to keep t