ty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence they are sunny.  \n   Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,c-1)\\in L^{(1)}_{c},\\qquad (c-1,1)\\in L^{(2)}_{c}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The set of points of \\(d_{c}\\) that have to be covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{c}:=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The two points displayed in (2) belong to \\(S_{c}\\); every other point \\((a,c-a)\\in S_{c}\\) with \\(a\\neq 1,c-1\\) also lies on the diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   d_{a+1}:\\;x+y=a+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently, after we **remove** the diagonal \\(d_{c}\\) and **add** the two sunny lines \\(L^{(1)}_{c},L^{(2)}_{c}\\), all points of \\(S_{c}\\) remain covered: the two special points are covered by the new sunny lines, the remaining points are covered by the diagonals appearing in (3).\n\n   The replacement (1) therefore **increases the number of sunny lines by 2 while increasing the total number of lines by 1** (one diagonal disappears, two new lines appear).\n\n3. **Balancing the total number of lines.**  \n   To keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) we pair each replacement described in step 2 with a **single‑line replacement** of the smallest diagonal \\(d_{2}\\) (which consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\)).  \n   We replace \\(d_{2}\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{2}:\\; y = x ,\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through \\((1,1)\\).  \n   This operation **removes one diagonal and adds one sunny line**, so the total number of lines does **not** change.\n\n   Performing the pair “replace \\(d_{c}\\) by the two lines (1) and replace \\(d_{2}\\) by (4)” therefore **adds exactly two sunny lines while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\)** (the net change in the line count is \\(+2-1-1=0\\)).\n\n4. **Inductive construction of any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n\n   *Start.* Begin with the base family \\(\\mathcal D\\) (which contains \\(0\\) sunny lines).\n\n   *Step.* Suppose we have already performed the paired replacement for the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   d_{c_{1}},d_{c_{2}},\\dots ,d_{c_{t}}\\qquad (c_{i}\\ge 3,\\;c_{1}<c_{2}<\\dots <c_{t}),\n   \\]\n\n   and consequently we have introduced exactly \\(2t\\) sunny lines while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \n   The remaining diagonals are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\\setminus\\{d_{c_{1}},\\dots ,d_{c_{t}}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Iteration.* Choose the smallest diagonal among the remaining ones whose index is at least \\(3\\); call it \\(d_{c}\\).  \n   Apply the paired replacement to \\(d_{c}\\) (add the two sunny lines (1) and replace the still‑present diagonal \\(d_{2}\\) by the sunny line (4)).  \n   Because we always use the **smallest** still‑available diagonal with index \\(\\ge 3\\), every point \\((a,c-a)\\) that is needed for the covering argument (3) lies on a diagonal whose index is **larger** than \\(c\\) and therefore has not been removed yet. Hence the covering argument of step 2 remains valid after each iteration.\n\n   Repeating this procedure \\(t\\) times we obtain a family consisting of  \n\n   * the \\(n-t\\) diagonals that have never been removed (these are non‑sunny), and  \n   * \\(2t\\) sunny lines (the two lines for each of the \\(t\\) replaced diagonals) together with the single sunny line that repeatedly replaces \\(d_{2}\\) (which is already counted among the \\(2t\\) because each pair contributes exactly two sunny lines).\n\n   Consequently the final family contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k = n-t\n   \\]\n\n   non‑sunny (i.e. not sunny) lines and \\(n-k = t\\) sunny lines, while the total number of lines is still \\(n\\).\n\n   Since \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), the number \\(k=n-t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Verification of the final family.**  \n\n   *Number of lines.* At each paired replacement the total number of lines stays unchanged; the initial family has \\(n\\) lines, therefore the final family also has \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Distinctness.* All diagonals are distinct.  \n   In each replacement the two newly introduced sunny lines have slopes \\(1\\) and \\(2\\), which are different from each other and from the slopes of any line introduced in a previous replacement (the same slopes are reused, but the lines are distinct because they pass through different points). Hence all lines in the final family are distinct.\n\n   *Sunny count.* Exactly the diagonals that have never been removed remain non‑sunny; their number is \\(k\\) by construction.\n\n   *Coverage.* Let \\((a,b)\\) be any point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   If the diagonal \\(d_{a+b}\\) has not been removed, the point lies on that diagonal and is covered.  \n   If \\(d_{a+b}\\) has been removed, then \\(a+b\\ge 3\\) and the paired replacement for \\(d_{a+b}\\) introduced the two sunny lines \\(L^{(1)}_{a+b}\\) and \\(L^{(2)}_{a+b}\\).  \n   By construction \\((a,b)\\) is either one of the two special points \\((1,a+b-1)\\) or \\((a+b-1,1)\\) (hence lies on a sunny line), or else \\(a\\neq 1\\) and \\(a\\neq a+b-1\\). In the latter case \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(d_{a+1}\\), whose index \\(a+1\\) is larger than \\(a+b\\) and therefore has not been removed at the moment when \\(d_{a+b}\\) was processed. Consequently \\(d_{a+1}\\) is still present in the final family and covers \\((a,b)\\).  \n\n   Thus every required lattice point belongs to at least one line of the final family.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), these are exactly the admissible values.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.