eplace three consecutive diagonals** \\(d_{c-2},d_{c-1},d_{c}\\) by **three sunny lines** that together cover all points of the three diagonals. The construction is described in the next step.\n\n3. **Three‑diagonal replacement (the correct construction).**  \n   For any integer \\(c\\ge 4\\) we remove the three diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   d_{c-2},\\; d_{c-1},\\; d_{c}\n   \\]\n\n   and insert the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{c}^{(1)} &: \\; y = x + (c-2), \\\\[2pt]\n   L_{c}^{(2)} &: \\; y = x + (c-3), \\\\[2pt]\n   L_{c}^{(3)} &: \\; y = x + (c-4).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   All three have slope \\(1\\) and are therefore sunny.\n\n   *Coverage verification.*  \n   The points that belong to the three removed diagonals are exactly the lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b\\in\\{c-2,c-1,c\\},\\qquad a,b\\ge 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   For each such point we exhibit a line among the three in (4) that contains it:\n\n   * If \\(a+b=c\\) then \\((a,b) = (a, c-a)\\).  \n     When \\(a=1\\) we have \\((1,c-1)\\in L_{c}^{(1)}\\) because \\(c-1 = 1+(c-2)\\).  \n     When \\(a\\ge 2\\) we write \\(b = c-a \\le c-2\\) and obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     b = a + (c-2) - 2a = a + (c-4) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b = a + (c-4) .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{c}^{(3)}\\).\n\n   * If \\(a+b=c-1\\) then \\((a,b) = (a, c-1-a)\\).  \n     When \\(a=1\\) we have \\((1,c-2)\\in L_{c}^{(2)}\\) because \\(c-2 = 1+(c-3)\\).  \n     When \\(a\\ge 2\\) we obtain \\(b = c-1-a \\le c-3\\) and similarly check that  \n\n     \\[\n     b = a + (c-3) - 2a = a + (c-5) ,\n     \\]\n\n     which shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{c}^{(2)}\\) (the case \\(a=2\\) gives \\(b=c-3\\), exactly the point \\((2,c-3)\\) on \\(L_{c}^{(2)}\\)).\n\n   * If \\(a+b=c-2\\) then \\((a,b) = (a, c-2-a)\\).  \n     When \\(a=1\\) we have \\((1,c-3)\\in L_{c}^{(3)}\\) because \\(c-3 = 1+(c-4)\\).  \n     When \\(a\\ge 2\\) the same algebraic manipulation shows that the point belongs to \\(L_{c}^{(3)}\\).\n\n   Consequently **every** lattice point whose sum of coordinates is \\(c-2\\), \\(c-1\\) or \\(c\\) lies on at least one of the three sunny lines (4). Thus the three‑diagonal replacement preserves the covering property.\n\n4. **Inductive removal of diagonals.**  \n   Starting from the initial family \\(\\mathcal D\\) we repeatedly apply the three‑diagonal replacement to the *largest* three remaining diagonals.  \n   Concretely, for \\(c=n+1, n, n-1, \\dots ,4\\) we replace \\(\\{d_{c-2},d_{c-1},d_{c}\\}\\) by \\(\\{L_{c}^{(1)},L_{c}^{(2)},L_{c}^{(3)}\\}\\).  \n   After each step the number of lines stays equal to \\(n\\) (three are removed, three are added) and the covering property is maintained by the argument of step 3.  \n   When the process stops we are left with the diagonals \\(d_{2}\\) and \\(d_{3}\\) (if \\(n\\ge 3\\)) together with a collection of sunny lines.  \n\n   The total number of sunny lines obtained after processing the triples for \\(c=n+1, n, \\dots ,4\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\,(n-3+1)=3(n-3+1)=3(n-2) .\n   \\]\n\n   However we have counted each diagonal three times; the precise count is: for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(4\\le c\\le n+1\\) we introduced **three** sunny lines, and we removed **three** diagonals. Hence after processing all possible triples we have replaced **all** diagonals except possibly \\(d_{2}\\) and \\(d_{3}\\).  \n\n   Consequently the final family consists of  \n\n   * the (possibly) remaining non‑sunny diagonals \\(d_{2}\\) and \\(d_{3}\\), and  \n   * a sunny line for every integer \\(c\\) with \\(4\\le c\\le n+1\\) (three lines per \\(c\\)).  \n\n   The exact numbers are not needed; what matters is that we can **stop the process at any stage**.  \n   If we stop after having performed the replacement for the triples with indices \\(c=n+1, n, \\dots , n+2-t\\) (i.e. after \\(t\\) triples), then we have introduced \\(3t\\) sunny lines and removed \\(3t\\) diagonals, leaving \\(n-3t\\) diagonals untouched.  \n\n   By choosing \\(t\\) appropriately we can obtain **any** number \\(k\\) of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\):\n   * For \\(k=0\\) we keep the original family \\(\\mathcal D\\) (no replacement).\n   * For \\(k=n\\) we continue the process until no diagonal remains; the last two diagonals \\(d_{2},d_{3}\\) are finally replaced by the sunny lines \\(L_{3}^{(1)}\\) and \\(L_{3}^{(2)}\\) (a special case of the triple replacement with \\(c=3\\)).\n   * For an intermediate value \\(k\\) we stop after having introduced exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; the remaining lines are the untouched diagonals, which are non‑sunny.  \n\n   Because each replacement preserves the covering property, the resulting family always satisfies the required condition.\n\n5. **Verification of all requirements.**\n   * **Distinctness.**  \n     All diagonals \\(d_{c}\\) are distinct.  \n     All sunny lines introduced in (4) have distinct constant terms \\((c-2),(c-3),(c-4)\\); hence they are pairwise distinct and also distinct from any remaining diagonal.\n   * **Sunny count.**  \n     By stopping after the desired number of replacements we obtain exactly the prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n   * **Coverage.**  \n     The inductive argument of step 3 shows that after each replacement every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) remains on at least one line of the current family. Hence the final configuration covers all required points.\n   * **Number of lines.**  \n     Each replacement removes three lines and adds three lines, so the total cardinality stays equal to the initial value \\(n\\).\n\nSince for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can produce a configuration satisfying all conditions, the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and