            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or diagonal \\((x+y=c)\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(d_s\\) are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\) in this range, so \\((a,b)\\in d_s\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines \\(\\{d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\\) cover \\(T_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for \\(k=n\\)\n\nChoose a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for definiteness take \\(m=\\frac12\\).  \nDefine, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\frac{2i-1}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny and the lines are distinct because their intercepts differ.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\),\n\n\\[\nb=\\frac12 a+\\frac{2i-1}{2}\n\\iff\ni=b-\\frac12 a+\\frac12 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the right‑hand side of (3) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A key geometric observation\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(L\\) be any line (sunny or non‑sunny) and let \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \nThen \\(L\\) contains **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(x+y=s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=s\\}\\) is a diagonal line.  \nIf \\(L\\) is not that diagonal, the two lines intersect in at most one point, so at most one point of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(s\\) can lie on \\(L\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) itself is the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\), then the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L\\) are precisely those with sum \\(s\\); there is exactly one such point for each admissible pair \\((a,b)\\), i.e. at most one. ∎  \n\nConsequently **any line can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\) for each possible sum**, and because there are exactly \\(n\\) possible sums, every line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of intermediate values \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(0<k<n\\).  \nLet the sunny lines be \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) and the non‑sunny lines be \\(N_1,\\dots ,N_{n-k}\\).\n\nFor each sum \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consider the points of \\(T_n\\) having that sum.  \nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line can contribute **at most one** such point, and a non‑sunny line can also contribute at most one.  \nMoreover, if a sunny line already supplies a point with sum \\(s\\), then no non‑sunny line can supply another point with the same sum (otherwise two distinct points of the same sum would lie on the same non‑sunny line, contradicting Lemma 1).  \n\nHence for each sum \\(s\\) the total number of points of that sum that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{from a sunny line, if any})\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;(\\text{at most one from each non‑sunny line})\\le n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSumming (4) over the \\(n\\) possible sums yields the crude bound \\(|T_n|\\le n^{2}\\), which is not contradictory.  \nWe sharpen the estimate by observing that **a non‑sunny line can be essential for at most one sum**.\n\nIndeed, a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); the sums of these points are the consecutive integers \\(c+1,c+2,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \nIf a sunny line already provides the point with the smallest of these sums, say \\(c+1\\), then the vertical line cannot provide any further point, because any other point on the same vertical line would have a larger sum and would therefore have to be supplied by a sunny line as well (otherwise two points of the same vertical line would share the same sum, violating Lemma 1).  \nThus a vertical line can be the *only* provider for at most one sum.  \nExactly the same reasoning applies to horizontal and diagonal non‑sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is bounded by  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot n\\;+\\;(n-k)\\cdot 1 = kn + n - k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le kn + n - k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le k(n-1) .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(0<k<n\\) then \\(k\\le n-1\\), so the right‑hand side of (7) is at most \\(\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).  \nEquality would require \\(k=n-1\\) and would force every non‑sunny line to be essential for a distinct sum.  \nBut there are only three families of non‑sunny lines, and within each family all lines share the s