 line can appear** unless all \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny (the case \\(k=0\\)). Thus, if \\(0<k<n\\) we may assume that every non‑sunny line is either vertical or horizontal.\n\n6. **A parity argument for the mixed case.**  \n   Suppose \\(0<k<n\\) and all non‑sunny lines are vertical or horizontal.  \n   Each vertical line has a fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate, say \\(x=c\\); it meets the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) at the point \\((c,s-c)\\). As \\(s\\) runs through \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the \\(y\\)-coordinate runs through the set \\(\\{s-c\\}\\). Because the line is vertical, the \\(x\\)-coordinate is the same for all those points; consequently two distinct vertical lines cannot share a point of \\(T_n\\). The same holds for horizontal lines.\n\n   Hence the \\(n-k\\) vertical/horizontal lines together can cover at most \\(n-k\\) points of each diagonal sum. Together with the at most \\(k\\) points contributed by sunny lines (by (1)), the total number of points of a fixed sum \\(s\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-k)=n. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   But the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\), and for the largest sum \\(s=n+1\\) we have \\(s-1=n\\). Thus (4) is tight for \\(s=n+1\\) but for any smaller sum \\(s\\) we have \\(s-1<n\\). In order to cover **all** points of the smaller diagonals we would need *strictly fewer* than \\(n\\) points per sum, which is impossible because each sunny line already uses up one of the available “slots’’ on that sum (Lemma 1). The only way to avoid a shortfall is that **every** sum is covered exactly as in (4), i.e. each sunny line must meet every diagonal, and each vertical/horizontal line must also meet every diagonal. This forces every vertical line to have a distinct \\(x\\)-coordinate and every horizontal line a distinct \\(y\\)-coordinate, giving at least \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines – contradicting the assumption \\(0<k<n\\).\n\n   Therefore a configuration with both sunny and non‑sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only feasible values of \\(k\\) are the two extremal cases already exhibited:\n\n   * \\(k=0\\): the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\);  \n   * \\(k=n\\): the \\(n\\) parallel sunny lines \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{2i-1}{2}\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n)\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }k=0\\text{ or }k=n.}\n\\]"
