(n+1\\) is not explicitly stated.\n* **Location:** Overall counting argument in Sections 4–7.  \n  * **Issue:** Justification Gap – the transition from the definitions of \\(a_s\\) to the global bounds on \\(k\\) relies on several informal “capacity” arguments that are not made precise; the proof does not rigorously exclude all intermediate values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Verification Log\n\nBelow each quoted passage I give a brief assessment of its correctness or explain the gap.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Notation and constructions (Steps 1–2)\n\n*Quote:*  \n> “\\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\ |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).”\n\n*Analysis:* Correct definition; the cardinality formula follows from a standard triangular count. No issue.\n\n*Quote:*  \n> “\\(k=0\\). The \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) are distinct, non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).”\n\n*Analysis:* Each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=s\\); the set of \\(s\\) values exactly matches the definition of \\(T_n\\). This construction is valid.\n\n*Quote:*  \n> “\\(k=n\\). … \\(\\ell_i: y=\\frac12x+\\frac{i}{2}\\) (i=1,…,n). … for \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(i=2b-a\\) and \\(1\\le 2b-a\\le n\\).”\n\n*Analysis:* The algebraic manipulation is correct; the inequalities follow from \\(1\\le a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the \\(\\ell_i\\). The lines have slope \\(1/2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so they are sunny. This construction is sound.\n\n*Conclusion:* Steps 1–2 are rigorous and correct.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Lemma 1 (Step 3)\n\n*Quote:*  \n> “Let \\(L\\) be any line that is **not** parallel to the family of diagonals \\(x+y=s\\). … the system … has at most one solution \\((x,y)\\) in \\(\\mathbb R^2\\); consequently \\(L\\) contains **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(x+y=s\\).”\n\n*Analysis:* Solving two non‑parallel linear equations